    "text": "Define EVIDENCE.  Mention The Types Of Medical Evidences . Write In Detail About Medical Evidences That Can Be Produced In The Court Of Law.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Legal System & Medical Jurisprudence",
    "answer": "### Definition of Evidence\nAccording to **Section 3 of the Indian Evidence Act**, evidence refers to and includes:\n1.  All statements which the court permits or requires to be made before it by witnesses, in relation to matters of fact under inquiry (known as **Oral Evidence**).\n2.  All documents, including electronic records, produced for the inspection of the court (known as **Documentary Evidence**).\n\n---\n\n### Classification of Medical Evidence\nMedical evidence is broadly divided into two main categories:\n1.  **Oral Medical Evidence**: Verbal statements made by a medical professional under oath in a court room, explaining their observations, clinical findings, or expert opinions.\n2.  **Documentary Medical Evidence**: Written records, certificates, and reports compiled by a registered medical practitioner during the course of their professional duties.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Medical Evidences Produced in Court\n\nSeveral key documentary medical evidences are highly valued in judicial proceedings:\n\n*   **Medical Certificates**: These are simple documents issued by a doctor certifying facts such as ill-health, fitness, age, vaccination, or psychiatric stability.\n*   **Medico-Legal Reports**: Formally prepared by a doctor upon a written requisition from a police officer or magistrate. These include:\n    *   *Injury Reports*: Detailing the nature, size, and age of wounds.\n    *   *Post-Mortem Reports*: Detailing internal and external autopsy findings to establish the cause of death.\n    *   *Sexual Assault Reports*: Forensic examination details of the victim and the accused.\n    *   *Drunkenness Certificates*: Assessing blood alcohol levels and clinical coordination.\n*   **Dying Declaration**: A statement made by a dying person explaining the cause or circumstances of their impending death (Section 32(1) of the Indian Evidence Act). It has immense legal weight.\n*   **Chemical Examiner's Report**: Analytical reports of viscera, blood stains, semen, or weapon swabs provided by forensic science laboratories.\n*   **Hospital Records**: Original case sheets, admission charts, surgical notes, temperature logs, and nursing charts which can be summoned to verify treatment details and timelines in cases of medical negligence.",
