    "text": "Classify Criminal Offences. Diff between Cognizable Offence & Non-cognizable .",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Legal System & Medical Jurisprudence",
    "answer": "### Classification of Criminal Offences\n\nUnder the **Code of Criminal Procedure (CrPC)**, criminal offences are systematically classified into three major categories based on their gravity, bail eligibility, and trial procedures:\n1.  **Cognizable and Non-Cognizable Offences** (based on police authority to arrest without a warrant).\n2.  **Bailable and Non-Bailable Offences** (based on whether bail is a matter of right or judicial discretion).\n3.  **Summons Cases and Warrant Cases** (based on the severity of the punishment and the complexity of the trial procedure).\n\n---\n\n### Differences between Cognizable and Non-Cognizable Offences\n\n| Feature | Cognizable Offence | Non-Cognizable Offence |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Legal Definition** | Defined under **Section 2(c)** of the CrPC. | Defined under **Section 2(l)** of the CrPC. |\n| **Power of Arrest** | A police officer can arrest the accused **without a warrant** from a magistrate. | A police officer has **no authority to arrest** without a valid warrant. |\n| **Initiation of Investigation** | The police can start an investigation immediately **without needing permission** from a magistrate. | The police **cannot investigate** the case without a formal order from a magistrate. |\n| **Nature of Crime** | Serious, public, and grave offences. | Less serious, private, and minor offences. |\n| **Examples** | Murder, Rape, Dowry Death, Kidnapping, and Theft. | Simple Hurt, Defamation, Assault, and Cheating. |\n| **First Step** | Begins with the registration of a **First Information Report (FIR)** under Section 154 CrPC. | Begins with the registration of a **Non-Cognizable Report (NCR)** under Section 155 CrPC. |\n| **Duty of Police** | The state is directly concerned, and the police have a duty to prevent and investigate such crimes proactively. | The police act as a passive registry and refer the complainant to the magistrate's court for further action. |",
