    "text": "Write About Witness (Expert Witness ,Hostile Witness).",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Legal System & Medical Jurisprudence",
    "answer": "### Definition of a Witness\n\nA **witness** is an individual who gives evidence in a court of law under oath or solemn affirmation, helping the court to determine the truth of a case. In forensic medicine and legal jurisprudence, two categories of witnesses are of critical importance: **Expert Witnesses** and **Hostile Witnesses**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Expert Witness\n\nAn **Expert Witness** is a person who possesses specialized scientific, technical, or professional training, skills, and experience in a specific field (such as medicine, ballistics, toxicology, or handwriting) and is called by the court to assist in interpreting technical evidence.\n*   **Legal Standing**: Defined under **Section 45 of the Indian Evidence Act**.\n*   **Key Distinction**: Unlike a common witness (who can only testify about facts directly observed through their five senses), an expert witness is legally permitted to express professional **opinions, deductions, and conclusions** based on observed or hypothetical facts.\n*   **Role**: A forensic pathologist testifying about the cause, weapon, and time since death based on autopsy findings is a classic example of an expert witness.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Hostile Witness\n\nA **Hostile Witness** is a witness who, while testifying in the witness box, displays a temperament contrary to the party that called them, deliberately suppresses the truth, or goes back on (retracts) their previously recorded statement given to the police.\n*   **Legal Standing**: Governed by **Section 154 of the Indian Evidence Act**.\n*   **Procedure**: When a witness turns hostile, the advocate who summoned them can request the judge to officially declare the witness \"hostile.\"\n*   **Consequences**: Once declared hostile, the advocate of the calling party is legally permitted to cross-examine their own witness and ask them **leading questions** to impeach their credibility and extract the truth.",
