    "chapter": "Legal System & Medical Jurisprudence",
    "answer": "### Perjury: Legal Definition and Consequences\n\n**Perjury** is the willful and deliberate giving of false evidence, or making a false statement under a legally binding oath or solemn affirmation, in a court of law or during a judicial proceeding. Under **Section 191 of the Indian Penal Code (IPC)**, this is defined as giving false evidence.\n\n**Punishment**:\nAccording to **Section 193 IPC**, the punishment for committing perjury in a judicial proceeding is rigorous imprisonment for a term which may extend to **seven years**, along with a fine. If committed in any other case, the punishment is up to three years of imprisonment and a fine. \n\nFor a medical professional, committing perjury by falsifying injury or autopsy reports is not only a severe criminal offense but also constitutes gross professional misconduct, leading to the permanent removal of their license (penal erasure).",
