    "chapter": "Legal System & Medical Jurisprudence",
    "answer": "### Leading Questions: Definition and Rules\n\nUnder **Section 141 of the Indian Evidence Act**, a **leading question** is defined as any question that suggests the specific answer which the person putting it wishes or expects to receive. These questions are typically structured to elicit a simple \"Yes\" or \"No\" response.\n\n**Legal Application**:\n*   **When Not Allowed**: Under Section 142, leading questions **cannot** be asked during the **Examination-in-Chief** or **Re-examination** without the explicit permission of the judge, except for introductory or undisputed facts.\n*   **When Allowed**: Under Section 143, leading questions are freely permitted during **Cross-examination** to test the witness's veracity.\n*   *Example*: \"Was the victim stabbed with this knife?\" is a leading question. \"What kind of weapon was used to inflict this wound?\" is a non-leading question.",
