    "chapter": "Medical Law & Ethics",
    "answer": "### Introduction to the National Medical Commission (NMC)\n\nThe **National Medical Commission (NMC)** was established by the Government of India under the National Medical Commission Act, 2019. It replaced the historic Medical Council of India (MCI) on September 25, 2020. The primary objective of the NMC is to improve access to quality medical education, ensure the availability of high-quality medical professionals, and enforce strict ethical standards in medical practice.\n\n---\n\n### Core Functions and Role of the NMC\n\nThe NMC operates through four autonomous boards and performs several vital functions:\n\n1.  **Regulating Medical Education**: It lays down state-of-the-art standards for infrastructure, faculty, and curriculum for both undergraduate (MBBS) and postgraduate (MD/MS/DM) medical courses across India. It is governed by the Under-Graduate and Post-Graduate Medical Education Boards.\n2.  **Assessment and Rating**: Through the Medical Assessment and Rating Board (MARB), it assesses and rates medical colleges, grants permission for starting new colleges, and approves increases in seat capacities.\n3.  **Maintaining the National Register**: The Ethics and Medical Registration Board (EMRB) maintains the **National Medical Register (NMR)**, which contains the names, qualifications, and registration details of all licensed medical practitioners in India.\n4.  **Enforcing Professional Ethics**: The EMRB drafts, updates, and implements code of conduct and professional ethics regulations. It acts as an appellate authority for cases of professional misconduct.\n5.  **Conducting Uniform Exams**: The NMC is responsible for implementing standard examinations like the National Exit Test (NExT), which serves as a licensing exam and a PG entrance exam.\n6.  **Fee Regulation**: The NMC drafts guidelines for determining fees for 50% of the seats in private medical institutions and deemed universities, ensuring affordable access to medical education.",
