    "id": 21,
    "text": "Types of Consent",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Medical Law & Ethics",
    "answer": "### Definition of Consent in Medical Practice\n\nIn medical jurisprudence, **consent** is defined as the voluntary agreement, compliance, or permission given by a patient for a clinical examination, diagnostic test, treatment, or surgical procedure. Under **Section 90 of the Indian Penal Code (IPC)**, consent is invalid if it is obtained under fear of injury, misrepresentation of facts, coercion, or from a person who is mentally unsound, intoxicated, or under **12 years of age**.\n\n---\n\n### Types of Consent\n\nMedical consent is broadly classified into the following types:\n\n#### 1. Implied Consent\nThis is the most common form of consent, expressed by the patient's passive actions rather than written or spoken words.\n*   *Example*: A patient walking into a doctor's chamber and sitting on the examination chair, or rolling up their sleeve and extending their arm for a venipuncture.\n\n#### 2. Expressed Consent\nThis is explicitly given by the patient, either verbally or in writing, for minor or semi-invasive procedures.\n*   *Oral Consent*: The patient verbally agrees to a procedure (e.g., incision and drainage) in the presence of a witness, which the doctor then documents in the case sheet.\n*   *Written Consent*: The patient signs a formal consent form. This is legally mandatory for all major surgeries, administration of general anesthesia, and invasive diagnostic tests.\n\n#### 3. Informed Consent\nThis is a highly structured process where the doctor explains the diagnosis, the nature of the proposed procedure, the inherent risks, potential complications, alternative treatment options, and the consequences of refusing treatment. The patient then gives voluntary, written consent.\n\n#### 4. Proxy (Surrogate) Consent\nGiven by a parent, legal guardian, or next of kin for individuals who are legally incompetent to give consent, such as minors (under 12 years) or comatose patients.",
