    "text": "Write About RES IPSA LOQUITUR",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Medical Law & Ethics",
    "answer": "### Res Ipsa Loquitur: Definition and Application\n\n**Res Ipsa Loquitur** is a Latin legal maxim meaning **\"the thing speaks for itself.\"** In medical negligence trials, it is a rule of evidence where the negligence is so glaringly obvious and undeniable that the plaintiff is not required to provide any expert testimony or separate proof of negligence.\n\n**Three Essential Conditions**:\n1.  The injury is of a type that does not ordinarily occur in the absence of negligence.\n2.  The instrument or agent causing the injury was under the exclusive control of the doctor.\n3.  The patient was entirely cooperative and did not contribute to the injury.\n\n*Examples*: Leaving a surgical scissor or sponge inside the patient's abdomen, amputating the wrong limb, or transfusing incompatible blood. Under this doctrine, the **burden of proof shifts** to the doctor to prove they were not negligent.",
