    "answer": "### Malingering: Definition and Examples\n\n**Malingering** is the deliberate, conscious, and motivated feigning, imitation, or gross exaggeration of physical or psychiatric symptoms, illnesses, or disabilities, aimed at achieving a specific external incentive or secondary gain.\n\n**Common Motives**:\nAvoiding criminal prosecution (using the insanity defense), evading military service, obtaining financial compensation (insurance fraud, personal injury claims), or escaping school/work.\n\n**Examples of Malingering**:\n1.  Pretending to have severe back pain or neck stiffness (whiplash) after a minor vehicle collision to claim high insurance payouts.\n2.  Feigning sudden blindness, deafness, or mutism.\n3.  Simulating hallucinations or severe amnesia to escape a murder trial.\n4.  Artificially raising body temperature by holding a thermometer against hot water, or self-inflicting minor abrasions and claiming they were homicidal injuries.",
