    "id": 32,
    "text": "Add A Note On GUSTAFSONS Changes In The Teeth And Its MLI",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Thanatology & Identity",
    "answer": "### Introduction to Gustafson's Method\n\n**Gustafson's Method** (introduced in 1950) is a well-established and highly reliable scientific technique used in forensic odontology to estimate the chronological age of an adult individual (above 21 years) by examining the structural, age-related changes in a single extracted tooth.\n\n---\n\n### The Six Gustafson's Criteria (A-S-C-R-T-P)\n\nThe method evaluates six distinct physiological changes in the dental tissue, which are easily observed in ground sections of the tooth:\n\n1.  **Attrition (A)**: The gradual wearing down of the enamel and dentin on the incisal or occlusal surfaces caused by mastication.\n2.  **Secondary Dentin (S)**: The physiological deposition of dentin within the pulp cavity over time, which reduces the size of the pulp chamber.\n3.  **Cementum Apposition (C)**: The continuous deposition of cementum layers at the root apex.\n4.  **Root Resorption (R)**: The gradual loss of cementum and dentin at the root tip, which becomes more pronounced in older age.\n5.  **Transparency of Dentin (T)**: The sclerosis and mineralization of dentinal tubules starting at the root apex and progressing upwards. This is the **most reliable** single indicator of age.\n6.  **Periodontosis (P)**: The gradual recession of the gum line (gingival recession) exposing the neck of the tooth.\n\n---\n\n### Scoring and Age Estimation Formula\n\nEach of these six parameters is graded on a scale of **0 (no change), 1 (minor change), 2 (moderate change), to 3 (maximum change)**. The scores of all six parameters are summed to get a total score ($X$). This total score is then plugged into a standard regression formula:\n$$Y = a + bX$$\nwhere $Y$ is the estimated age of the individual, and $a$ and $b$ are constants calculated from a reference population.\n\n---\n\n### Medico-Legal Importance\n\n*   **Age Estimation in Adults**: It is the preferred method for estimating the age of skeletal remains, highly decomposed bodies, or victims of mass disasters (like plane crashes or fires).\n*   **Forensic Accuracy**: It provides high accuracy, with an error margin of only $\\pm 5$ to $\\pm 10$ years in experienced hands.",
