    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Thanatology & Identity",
    "answer": "### Definition and Principles of Dactylography\n\n**Dactylography** (also known as Dactyloscopy, Dermatoglyphics, or Galton's Fingerprint System) is the study of the epidermal ridge patterns on the pads of the fingers and thumbs for the purpose of absolute identification. It is based on two fundamental scientific principles:\n1.  **Absolute Uniqueness**: No two individuals, including identical (monozygotic) twins, have identical fingerprint ridge characteristics.\n2.  **Permanence**: The ridge patterns develop during the fourth month of intrauterine life and remain completely unchanged throughout the individual's life until decomposition sets in after death.\n\n---\n\n### Types of Fingerprint Patterns (Galton's Classification)\n\nFingerprints are classified into four primary patterns based on the flow of ridges:\n\n1.  **Loops (60-65% of cases)**: Ridges enter from one side of the finger, curve back, and exit from the same side. They are sub-classified as Ulnar or Radial loops.\n2.  **Whorls (25-30% of cases)**: Ridges are arranged in a circular, spiral, or concentric pattern.\n3.  **Arches (5-7% of cases)**: The simplest pattern, where ridges enter from one side and exit on the opposite side, forming a slight wave or arch.\n4.  **Composites (1-2% of cases)**: Complex patterns that combine two or more of the above patterns (such as lateral pocket loops or accidental patterns).\n\n---\n\n### Impairment and Alterations\n\nEpidermal ridge patterns are highly resilient. They cannot be permanently altered or erased by superficial injuries, abrasions, or mild chemical burns. The ridges will regrow in their exact original pattern once the skin heals. \n\nPermanent impairment only occurs if the deep **dermal layer** is severely damaged, resulting in scar tissue. This occurs in third-degree burns, deep lacerations, leprosy, or active surgical skin grafting.\n\n---\n\n### Medico-Legal Importance (MLI)\n\n*   **Absolute Identification**: Serves as the most reliable, legally accepted method for identifying criminals, fugitives, and victims of mass disasters.\n*   **Solving Crimes**: Analyzing latent (invisible) or patent (visible) prints left at a crime scene to identify the perpetrator.\n*   **Civil Verification**: Preventing forgery and verifying identity on deeds, wills, bank documents, passports, and digital biometric systems.",
