    "text": "Add A Note On Mixed Dentition.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Thanatology & Identity",
    "answer": "### Mixed Dentition: Characteristics and MLI\n\n**Mixed Dentition** refers to the developmental period in a child's life when both temporary (deciduous) and permanent teeth are simultaneously present in the oral cavity.\n\n**Key Clinical Features**:\n*   **Age Range**: This period spans from approximately **6 to 12 years** of age.\n*   It begins at around 6 years with the eruption of the first permanent molar behind the deciduous molars.\n*   It ends around 12 years when the last deciduous tooth (usually the second deciduous molar) is shed and replaced by the permanent premolars and canines.\n\n**Medico-Legal Importance**:\nMixed dentition is a highly reliable physiological marker for estimating the chronological age of living children. This is crucial for verifying school admissions, assessing criminal responsibility (juvenile status), detecting child labor violations, and identifying skeletal remains of children.",
