    "text": "What Is Skull Super Imposition.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Thanatology & Identity",
    "answer": "### Skull Superimposition: Definition and Forensic Value\n\n**Skull Superimposition** (or Video-Photo Superimposition) is a forensic identification technique used to determine if skeletal remains (specifically the skull) belong to a missing person whose photograph is available.\n\n**Methodology**:\nA life-sized photograph of the face of the missing person is superimposed over a photograph or 3D digital scan of the unidentified skull. The two images are aligned and compared at identical, stable anatomical landmarks, such as the outer and inner canthus of the eyes, the nasion (bridge of nose), the gnathion (chin), and the external auditory meatus.\n\n**Medico-Legal Importance**:\n*   It is primarily a tool of **exclusion**. If the bony landmarks do not align with the facial features, the skull is definitely excluded.\n*   If they align perfectly, it indicates a high probability of identity, which must be confirmed using DNA profiling or dental records.",
