    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Thanatology & Identity",
    "answer": "### Barr Body: Definition and Medico-Legal Importance\n\nA **Barr Body** (or sex chromatin) is a small, condensed, dark-staining planoconvex structure representing an inactive X chromosome. It is located against the inner nuclear membrane in the somatic cells of females.\n\n**Forensic Detection**:\nIt is commonly analyzed by taking buccal smears, hair root sheaths, or peripheral blood smears (where they appear as \"drumsticks\" in 2-6% of neutrophils).\n\n**Medico-Legal Importance (MLI)**:\n1.  **Sex Determination**: It is a rapid and highly reliable method to determine the biological sex of highly decomposed bodies, mutilated remains, or tissue fragments found at crime scenes.\n2.  **DSD Diagnosis**: It helps identify chromosomal sex abnormalities. For instance, a Barr body is **absent** in females with Turner syndrome (45, XO) and **present** in males with Klinefelter syndrome (47, XXY).",
