    "text": "Anthropometry",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Thanatology & Identity",
    "answer": "### Anthropometry: Definition and Medico-Legal Importance\n\n**Anthropometry** is the systematic study and recording of physical measurements of the human body. Historically, Alphonse Bertillon developed the first scientific identification system based on anthropometry in 1882, known as **Bertillonage**. This system relied on the fact that stable bony dimensions (such as height, head length, arm span, and finger length) do not change after 21 years of age.\n\n**Modern Medico-Legal Applications**:\n*   **Forensic Anthropology**: Used to estimate the stature, age, sex, and race of an individual from skeletal remains, long bones, or skull fragments.\n*   **Stature Estimation**: Standard mathematical regression formulae (such as Karl Pearson's or Trotter & Gleser's) are applied to the length of long bones (like femur or humerus) to estimate the living height of the deceased.\n*   **Biometrics and Surveillance**: Modern digital photogrammetry uses anthropometry to identify suspects from CCTV footage by analyzing body proportions.",
