    "text": "MLI of SCARS",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Thanatology & Identity",
    "answer": "### Scars: Definition and Medico-Legal Importance\n\nA **Scar** is a permanent, avascular fibrous tissue deposit that forms in the dermis as the final result of the healing process of a wound, surgical incision, or deep disease process.\n\n**Medico-Legal Importance (MLI)**:\n1.  **Personal Identification**: Scars are permanent and unique to an individual, making them excellent markers for identifying living suspects or dead bodies.\n2.  **Nature of the Weapon**: The shape of the scar often reflects the original wound. A linear scar suggests a sharp-edged weapon, while an irregular, broad scar indicates a laceration.\n3.  **Age of the Injury**: The color and texture of the scar change over time (transitioning from reddish-pink to pigmented, and finally to a silvery-white, contracted appearance), helping estimate the time elapsed since the injury.\n4.  **Intention**: Suicidal scars are typically multiple and linear on the front of the left wrist; defense scars are common on the extensor aspects of the forearms.",
