    "text": "Write in detail About EXHUMATION",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Medico-Legal Autopsy",
    "answer": "### EXHUMATION\n\n**Definition**\nExhumation is the lawful excavation and retrieval of a buried body from its grave for the purpose of a postmortem examination or re-examination to determine the cause of death, establish identity, or gather evidence in criminal cases.\n\n**Legal Authority**\n* In India, exhumation can only be ordered by an Executive Magistrate (such as a District Magistrate, Sub-divisional Magistrate, or Taluka Magistrate) under **Section 176 of the CrPC** (now relevant provisions of Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita).\n* A police officer or a medical officer has no authority to order an exhumation on their own.\n\n**Time Limit**\n* In India, there is **no time limit** for exhumation. A body can be exhumed at any time after burial if there is suspicion of foul play.\n* Although soft tissues decompose, valuable evidence like skeletal injuries (fractures), dental records, and certain non-volatile poisons (such as arsenic, antimony, lead, and copper) remain detectable for years or decades.\n\n**Procedure**\n1. **Time and Presence**: The exhumation must be conducted in daylight, preferably early morning, under the direct supervision of the Executive Magistrate, the Police officer, and the Medical Officer.\n2. **Identification**: The grave must be identified by the cemetery registrar, grave-digger, or relatives.\n3. **Control of Environment**: The area should be cordoned off to prevent public intrusion and preserve trace evidence. Disinfectants should *never* be sprayed on the grave, as they may contaminate toxicological samples.\n4. **Soil Sampling**: Before reaching the coffin/body, control soil samples (approx. 500g each) must be collected from above, below, and adjacent to the grave to rule out environmental contamination in suspected poisoning cases.\n5. **Autopsy**: Once exhumed, the body is identified by relatives. An autopsy is conducted on-site inside a temporary screen or at the nearest mortuary if possible. Viscera, bones, hair, and nails are preserved for chemical analysis.",
