    "text": "Incisions at Autopsy.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Medico-Legal Autopsy",
    "answer": "### Incisions at Autopsy\n\nDuring a medico-legal autopsy, standard skin incisions are utilized to systematically open the thoracic, abdominal, and cranial cavities. The choice of incision depends on the nature of the case, the area of suspected trauma, and cosmetic considerations.\n\n**Primary Body Incisions**\n1. **I-Shaped (Midline) Incision**:\n   * **Technique**: This is the most common incision. It starts from the level of the thyroid cartilage or the chin, runs vertically down the midline over the sternum and abdomen, curves slightly to the left side of the umbilicus (to avoid the ligamentum teres), and terminates at the symphysis pubis.\n   * **Indication**: Used routinely in standard autopsies for comprehensive exposure of the thoracic and abdominal cavities.\n2. **Y-Shaped Incision**:\n   * **Technique**: Commences from the mastoid process behind both ears, runs down the lateral sides of the neck to meet at the suprasternal notch, and then extends down the midline of the chest and abdomen to the symphysis pubis.\n   * **Indication**: Preferred when extensive dissection of the neck is required (e.g., hanging, manual strangulation, or throttling) to expose the deep structures without distortion.\n3. **Modified Y-Shaped Incision**:\n   * **Technique**: Starts from the acromion processes (shoulders) on both sides, curves downwards and medially below the breasts to meet at the xiphoid process of the sternum, and then runs down the midline to the pubic symphysis.\n   * **Indication**: Preferred in female bodies for cosmetic reasons, as the incision is hidden and does not disfigure the upper chest.\n4. **T-Shaped Incision**:\n   * **Technique**: A horizontal incision is made from shoulder to shoulder, and a vertical incision is made down the midline from the sternal notch to the pubis.\n\n**Cranial Incision**\n* **Bi-mastoid Coronal Incision**: To open the skull, a coronal incision is made across the vertex of the skull from one mastoid process (behind the ear) to the other. The scalp is then reflected forward over the face and backward over the occiput, exposing the skull cap (calvarium) for sawing.",
