    "id": 46,
    "text": "Preservation of Viscera in Poisoning Case.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Medico-Legal Autopsy",
    "answer": "### Preservation of Viscera in Poisoning Cases\n\nWhen poisoning is suspected during a medico-legal autopsy, specific viscera and body fluids must be preserved and sent to the Forensic Science Laboratory (FSL) for chemical analysis.\n\n**Viscera to be Preserved**\n1. **Stomach and its Contents**: The entire stomach along with its complete contents must be preserved.\n2. **Small Intestines**: The upper part of the small intestine (first 30 cm of duodenum and jejunum) along with its contents.\n3. **Liver**: At least 500 grams of the liver (preferably the right lobe) to detect metabolized poisons.\n4. **Kidneys**: Half of each kidney (longitudinally cut).\n5. **Spleen**: Half of the spleen.\n6. **Blood**: 50 to 100 ml of blood collected from peripheral veins (femoral or internal jugular) to detect alcohol, drugs, or carbon monoxide.\n7. **Urine**: All available urine in the bladder.\n\n**Preservatives Used**\n* **Saturated Solution of Common Salt (Sodium Chloride)**:\n  * This is the standard, safest, and most widely used preservative in India.\n  * **Indications**: Used for all cases of poisoning, *except* suspected salt poisoning, corrosive acid, or corrosive alkali poisoning.\n* **Rectified Spirit (95% Alcohol)**:\n  * **Indications**: Preferred for organic, alkaloidal, and metallic poisons.\n  * **Contraindications**: Must *never* be used in cases of suspected poisoning by alcohol, ether, chloroform, chloral hydrate, phosphorus, phenol, or corrosive acids, because alcohol interferes with their chemical detection or dissolves them.\n\n**Mode of Sealing and Dispatch**\n1. Viscera are placed in clean, wide-mouthed glass jars (separately for stomach/intestines and liver/kidneys).\n2. The jars are filled up to two-thirds capacity to prevent pressure buildup of putrefactive gases.\n3. The jars are sealed with wax, bearing the medical officer's personal seal.\n4. Jars are labeled with case details and dispatched via a police constable to the FSL along with a copy of the autopsy report and specimen seal impression.",
