    "text": "Obscure autopsy.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Medico-Legal Autopsy",
    "answer": "### Obscure Autopsy\n\nAn **obscure autopsy** is one in which the gross and histopathological examinations of the body fail to reveal a clear, definitive anatomical cause of death, but the clinical history, surrounding circumstances, or minor autopsy findings strongly suggest a highly probable cause.\n\n**Common Causes and Contexts:**\n* Mild or early coronary artery disease with sudden collapse.\n* Reflex vagal inhibition (shock from minor trauma to trigger zones like the neck, glottis, or scrotum).\n* Heat stroke or hypothermia.\n* Status epilepticus or mild epilepsy (SUDEP - Sudden Unexpected Death in Epilepsy).\n* Severe bronchospasm or anaphylaxis.\n\nIn these cases, while the physical postmortem findings are minimal or borderline, they become legally and medically interpretable when correlated with circumstantial evidence, unlike a completely negative autopsy where no cause can be established even after full laboratory workup.",
