    "text": "Negtive Autopsy.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Medico-Legal Autopsy",
    "answer": "### Negative Autopsy\n\nA **negative autopsy** is defined as a postmortem examination in which, despite a meticulous external and internal examination, comprehensive toxicological analysis, detailed histopathological evaluation, and complete biochemical/microbiological testing, no anatomical or physiological cause of death can be determined.\n\n**Major Causes of Negative Autopsy:**\n1. **Functional Cardiac Abnormalities**: Channelopathies (e.g., Long QT syndrome, Brugada syndrome) or cardiac arrhythmias.\n2. **Vagal Inhibition**: Immediate cardiac arrest due to stimulation of the carotid sinus or laryngeal nerves (dry drowning, blow to the neck/scrotum).\n3. **Hyperacute Poisoning**: Poisons that act rapidly and leave no tissue changes, if not detected in toxicology.\n4. **Early Myocardial Infarction**: Death occurring within minutes of ischemia before visible necrosis or histological changes develop.\n5. **Mild Electrocution**: Where no electrical burns are present on the skin.\n6. **Concussive Brain Injury**: Direct trauma disrupting vital brainstem centers without gross hemorrhage.",
