    "text": "Methods of removal of organs during autopsy techniques.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Medico-Legal Autopsy",
    "answer": "### Methods of Organ Removal in Autopsy\n\nDuring an internal postmortem examination, there are four standard surgical methods used to remove internal organs from the body cavities:\n\n1. **Virchow’s Method**: Organs are removed one by one and dissected individually. It is the easiest and most common technique, but it completely destroys the anatomical relationships between different organ systems.\n2. **Rokitansky’s Method**: Organs are dissected *in-situ* (in their original positions) block-by-block. This is rarely used today except in highly infectious cases.\n3. **Ghon’s Method**: Organs are removed in functionally related anatomical blocks (e.g., thoracic block, abdominal block, urogenital block) and then dissected.\n4. **Letulle’s Method (En-Masse)**: All organs of the thoracic, abdominal, and pelvic cavities are removed together as a single massive block. This is the best method for preserving complex vascular connections, trauma tracts (e.g., stab or gunshot paths), and congenital anomalies.",
