    "id": 52,
    "text": "How is Stomach dissection done during Autopsy?",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Medico-Legal Autopsy",
    "answer": "### Dissection of the Stomach at Autopsy\n\nThe stomach must be carefully dissected to inspect for poisons, drugs, or pathology:\n\n1. **Isolation**: Two double ligatures (clamps or threads) are tied: one at the cardiac end (esophagogastric junction) and another at the pyloric end (duodenal junction) to prevent leakage of contents.\n2. **Removal**: The stomach is cut between the ligatures, removed, and placed in a clean, flat-bottomed forensic tray.\n3. **Opening**: It is opened along the **greater curvature** using bowel scissors, from the cardiac end to the pylorus.\n4. **Collection**: The gastric contents are poured into a graduated cylinder to record volume, color, odor, consistency, and presence of pill fragments or foreign material.\n5. **Inspection**: The empty stomach is washed gently under running water and the mucosal lining is examined for congestion, petechial hemorrhages (submucosal), ulcers, perforations, or corrosive damage.",
