    "text": "Dissection of Heart During Autopsy.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Medico-Legal Autopsy",
    "answer": "### Dissection of the Heart at Autopsy\n\nThe heart is dissected to evaluate congenital defects, valvular diseases, coronary occlusion, and myocardial infarction:\n\n1. **In-situ Inspection**: The pericardial sac is opened, and the heart is examined for fluid (pericardial effusion/tamponade) and external anomalies.\n2. **Inflow-Outflow Method**: The heart is dissected following the direction of blood flow:\n   * **Right Side**: Opened from the superior vena cava into the right atrium, down the lateral border of the right ventricle, and cut out through the pulmonary artery.\n   * **Left Side**: Opened from the pulmonary veins to the left atrium, down the lateral wall of the left ventricle to the apex, and cut out through the aortic valve.\n3. **Coronary Artery Examination**: The coronary arteries (LAD, circumflex, and right coronary) are transversely sectioned at 2-3 mm intervals along their course to check for atheromatous plaques or thrombosis.\n4. **Myocardium**: The ventricles are sliced horizontally (\"bread-loafing\") to detect areas of acute ischemia or old infarctions.",
