    "text": "Write about Sudden Natural deaths (Causes)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Thanatology",
    "answer": "### Sudden Natural Deaths\n\n**Definition**\nSudden death is defined as an unexpected, rapid, natural death occurring within 24 hours (often within minutes or a few hours) of the onset of acute symptoms, in an individual who was otherwise seemingly healthy or whose clinical illness was not considered terminal.\n\n**Causes by Physiological System**\nSudden natural deaths are classified by the organ system involved, based on Reddy's Textbook of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology:\n\n1. **Cardiovascular System (50–60% of cases)**:\n   * *Ischemic Heart Disease (IHD)*: Coronary artery atherosclerosis with or without acute thrombosis (most common cause).\n   * *Myocardial Infarction*: Acute ischemic necrosis of the heart muscle.\n   * *Hypertensive Heart Disease*: Cardiomegaly predisposing to fatal arrhythmias.\n   * *Ruptured Aortic Aneurysm*: Typically syphilitic or atherosclerotic aneurysms of the aorta dissecting or rupturing into the pericardial/pleural cavities.\n   * *Myocarditis & Cardiomyopathy*: (e.g., Hypertrophic Obstructive Cardiomyopathy - HOCM).\n2. **Respiratory System (15–20% of cases)**:\n   * *Massive Pulmonary Embolism*: Originating from deep vein thrombosis (DVT).\n   * *Lobar Pneumonia*: Unresolved acute bacterial infection.\n   * *Status Asthmaticus*: Acute, severe refractory bronchial asthma.\n   * *Massive Hemoptysis*: Rupture of a blood vessel in tuberculous cavities or bronchiectasis.\n3. **Central Nervous System (10–15% of cases)**:\n   * *Subarachnoid Hemorrhage*: Rupture of a congenital berry aneurysm in the Circle of Willis.\n   * *Cerebral Hemorrhage*: Stroke caused by rupture of Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms in hypertensive patients.\n   * *SUDEP*: Sudden Unexpected Death in Epilepsy.\n4. **Gastrointestinal & Genitourinary Systems (5–10% of cases)**:\n   * *Ruptured Esophageal Varices*: In liver cirrhosis patients with portal hypertension.\n   * *Perforated Peptic Ulcer*: Leading to acute peritonitis and septic shock.\n   * *Acute Hemorrhagic Pancreatitis*.\n   * *Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy*: Leading to massive hemoperitoneum.",
