    "text": "Bishops tripod of life.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Thanatology",
    "answer": "### Bishop's Tripod of Life\n\n**Bishop's Tripod of Life** is a physiological concept describing the three vital systems of the body that are absolutely essential for the maintenance of somatic life. These three systems form an interdependent, close-loop tripod:\n\n1. **The Circulatory System** (represented by the **Heart**).\n2. **The Respiratory System** (represented by the **Lungs**).\n3. **The Nervous System** (represented by the **Brain** and brain stem).\n\nBecause they are physiological partners, a permanent, irreversible failure of any single limb of this tripod inevitably and rapidly triggers the failure of the remaining two systems. This progression culminates in somatic death, followed by molecular death of the individual tissues. Understanding the tripod helps pathologists classify the mode of death as syncope (heart), asphyxia (lungs), or coma (brain).",
