    "text": "What are the manners of death?.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Thanatology",
    "answer": "### Manners of Death\n\nThe **manner of death** refers to the fashion, circumstances, and legal category under which the cause of death came into existence. It answers \"how\" the death occurred and is classified into five main categories:\n\n1. **Natural**: Death resulting solely from a natural disease process, illness, or aging (e.g., myocardial infarction, tuberculosis, senility) without external intervention.\n2. **Accidental**: Unnatural death resulting from an unforeseen, unintentional event or mishap (e.g., road traffic accidents, falls, drowning).\n3. **Suicidal**: Self-inflicted death where the deceased had the intent to end their own life (e.g., hanging, deliberate poisoning).\n4. **Homicidal**: Unnatural death caused by the willful, deliberate, or negligent act of another human being (e.g., stabbing, shooting, throttling).\n5. **Undetermined**: Classed when the autopsy findings, history, and investigation are insufficient to clarify the exact manner of death.",
