    "id": 63,
    "text": "Add a note on ALGOR MORTIS/postmortem cooling",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Postmortem Changes & Artefacts",
    "answer": "### Algor Mortis (Postmortem Cooling)\n\n**Definition**\nAlgor mortis is the progressive fall in the body's temperature after somatic death until it reaches equilibrium with the temperature of the surrounding environment.\n\n**Mechanism**\nUpon death, cellular metabolism stops, halting heat production. The body behaves as a physical mass, losing heat through the physical mechanisms of radiation, conduction, and convection.\n\n**Measurement**\nTo estimate the postmortem interval, the internal body temperature must be measured. This is done using a specialized chemical postmortem thermometer ($10 \\text{ to } 12 \\text{ inches}$ long, ranging from $0 \\text{ to } 50^\\circ\\text{C}$). It is inserted approximately $3 \\text{ to } 4 \\text{ inches}$ into the rectum or pushed directly into the substance of the liver through a subcostal abdominal puncture.\n\n**Rate of Cooling**\nThe temperature loss follows a **sigmoid curve** (showing a plateau phase for the first $1 \\text{ to } 3 \\text{ hours}$, followed by a rapid linear drop, and then a slow tail).\n* In temperate climates, the body cools at an average rate of $0.5 \\text{ to } 1.0^\\circ\\text{C}$ per hour.\n* In tropical countries like India, the rate is slower, averaging $0.4 \\text{ to } 0.7^\\circ\\text{C}$ per hour, because the ambient temperature is closer to body temperature.\n\n**Factors Affecting Algor Mortis:**\n1. **Environmental Temperature**: Rapid cooling occurs in cold, dry, or windy conditions.\n2. **Body Build and Size**: Children and thin/emaciated individuals cool faster than obese adults due to a larger surface-area-to-mass ratio and less insulating subcutaneous fat.\n3. **Clothing**: Heavy clothing insulates the body, slowing down the rate of cooling.\n4. **Cause of Death**: Conditions like tetanus, strychnine poisoning, or septicemia may show a temporary postmortem temperature rise (**postmortem caloricity**) before cooling begins, whereas wasting diseases accelerate cooling.\n\n**Medico-Legal Importance**\nIt is one of the most reliable methods for estimating the **Time Since Death (TSD)** in the first 24 hours:\n$$TSD = \\frac{\\text{Normal Body Temp (37}^\\circ\\text{C)} - \\text{Rectal Temp}}{\\text{Rate of cooling per hour}}$$",
    "stars": 4
