    "id": 64,
    "text": "Add a note on LIVOR MORTIS(postmortem Hypostasis/PM lividity)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Postmortem Changes & Artefacts",
    "answer": "### Livor Mortis (Postmortem Lividity or Hypostasis)\n\n**Definition**\nLivor mortis is a physical postmortem change characterized by a reddish-purple discoloration of the skin in the dependent (lowest) parts of the dead body. It is caused by the gravitational pooling of blood in the capillaries and venules once systemic circulation has ceased.\n\n**Chronology**\n* **Onset**: Starts as patchy, isolated spots within $1 \\text{ to } 2 \\text{ hours}$ after death.\n* **Fixation**: Lividity spreads, merges, and becomes fully developed and **fixed** in $6 \\text{ to } 12 \\text{ hours}$. Before fixation, pressing a thumb firmly on the area causes blanching (the blood shifts). Once fixed, the blood undergoes hemolysis and diffuses into surrounding tissues, so pressure no longer causes blanching.\n\n**Distribution**\nIt occurs in the lowest parts of the body relative to its position. However, it is absent in areas of direct contact with the ground or hard surfaces (e.g., shoulders, buttocks, heels in a supine body). This is called **contact pallor** or pressure blanching, as the compressed capillaries cannot fill with blood.\n\n**Medico-Legal Importance:**\n\n1. **Sure Sign of Death**: Confirms that somatic death has occurred.\n2. **Estimation of Time Since Death (TSD)**: Helps determine if death occurred $<2 \\text{ hours}$ ago (patchy), $2-6 \\text{ hours}$ ago (not fixed), or $>6-12 \\text{ hours}$ ago (fully fixed).\n3. **Position of the Body**: Indicates the posture of the deceased at the time of death. If a body is found supine but the lividity is on the abdomen (prone), it proves the body was turned or moved after lividity had fixed.\n4. **Indication of Cause of Death (Lividity Color)**:\n   * *Cherry Red*: Carbon monoxide poisoning.\n   * *Bright Red/Pink*: Cyanide poisoning or exposure to extreme cold (hypothermia).\n   * *Dark Brown/Chocolate*: Potassium chlorate, nitrites, or aniline poisoning (methaemoglobinaemia).\n   * *Bluish-Purple*: Asphyxial deaths (due to high reduced hemoglobin).\n   * *Pale/Faint*: Death due to massive internal or external hemorrhage.\n5. **Differentiation from a Bruise**: In hypostasis, an incision shows blood contained within vessels that washes away easily. In a bruise (contusion), blood is extravasated into the tissues, is clotted, and cannot be washed away.",
