    "id": 65,
    "text": "Add a Note on RIGOR MORTIS+ Nystens Rule   & Rule of 12",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Postmortem Changes & Artefacts",
    "answer": "### Rigor Mortis, Nysten's Rule, and the Rule of 12\n\n**Definition and Mechanism**\nRigor mortis is the postmortem stiffening of both voluntary and involuntary muscles of the body. Following death, during the primary flaccidity stage, muscles remain relaxed. However, as cell respiration stops, **Adenosine Triphosphate (ATP)** levels progressively deplete. Once ATP levels fall below $15\\%$ of normal, the actin and myosin filaments bind irreversibly to form rigid actomyosin complexes, locking the muscles in a contracted state. This stiffness remains until putrefaction breaks down the muscle proteins (secondary flaccidity).\n\n**Nysten’s Rule**\nNysten's Rule describes the specific, orderly progression in which rigor mortis appears and subsequently disappears in the body's skeletal muscles.\n* **Order of Appearance**: Rigor mortis is first observed in the myocardium (heart), followed by the involuntary muscles, and then the skeletal muscles in a downward sequence:\n  $$\\text{Myocardium} \\rightarrow \\text{Eyelids & Jaw (masseter)} \\rightarrow \\text{Neck & Face} \\rightarrow \\text{Trunk} \\rightarrow \\text{Upper Limbs (shoulder to fingers)} \\rightarrow \\text{Lower Limbs (hip to toes)}$$\n* **Order of Disappearance**: Rigor mortis passes off in the exact same downward sequence, starting from the face and jaw and leaving the lower extremities last.\n\n**The Rule of 12**\nIn tropical climates like India, the timeline of rigor mortis is highly variable due to high ambient temperatures, but it is classically summarized by the **Rule of 12**:\n* It takes **12 hours** to gradually set in and become fully established over the entire body.\n* It remains fully established for **12 hours**.\n* It takes another **12 hours** to gradually pass off and disappear completely.\n* The entire cycle of rigor mortis lasts for approximately **36 hours**.\n\n**Medico-Legal Importance**\n1. It is a definite, undeniable sign of death.\n2. It helps estimate the **Time Since Death (TSD)**.\n3. It indicates the position of the body at the time of death, provided it has not been disturbed.",
