    "text": "Write About Artifacts.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Postmortem Changes & Artefacts",
    "answer": "### Postmortem Artifacts\n\n**Definition**\nAn artifact in forensic pathology is defined as any change, finding, damage, or feature introduced to a dead body during the postmortem period (after death) that was not present during life. Artifacts can easily be misinterpreted as signs of ante-mortem trauma, natural disease, or violence, potentially leading to errors in the investigation.\n\n**Classification and Examples**\n\n1. **Agonal or Resuscitation-Related Artifacts**:\n   * *Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR)*: Can cause sternal or rib fractures, anterior chest wall bruising, and retrosternal hemorrhage, which must not be confused with homicidal chest compression.\n   * *Intubation and Therapy*: Postmortem gastric intubation can cause esophageal mucosal tears; IV lines can cause localized pooling of blood resembling bruises.\n2. **Decomposition-Related Artifacts**:\n   * *Postmortem Purge*: Decomposition gases force blood-stained, foul-smelling fluid out of the nose and mouth. This can be mistaken for pulmonary hemorrhage, drowning, or corrosive poisoning.\n   * *Marbling*: Reddish-brown branching lines along superficial veins (due to sulfhemoglobin formation) can mimic pattern bruising or strangulation marks.\n   * *Skin Slippage and Bloating*: Epidermal peeling can resemble scald burns, and bloating can mimic obesity or pregnancy.\n3. **Animal and Insect Activity**:\n   * *Postmortem Animal Bites*: Rats, mice, dogs, ants, cockroaches, or fish feed on soft tissues (lips, eyelids, fingers), producing jagged, dry wounds. These wounds lack a **vital reaction** (no tissue swelling, clotting, or active bleeding) and can be distinguished from homicidal mutilation.\n4. **Handling and Autopsy Artifacts**:\n   * *Rough Handling*: Dragging a body can cause postmortem abrasions (which are dry, yellow, and leathery).\n   * *Autopsy Incisions*: Extravasation of blood into tissue during autopsy can mimic ante-mortem bruises.\n\n**Medico-Legal Importance**\nPathologists must accurately identify and differentiate artifacts from genuine ante-mortem lesions to avoid misdiagnosing the cause and manner of death and to prevent the wrongful prosecution of innocent individuals.",
