    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Postmortem Changes & Artefacts",
    "answer": "### Marbling of the Skin\n\n**Definition and Mechanism:**\n**Marbling** is an early sign of putrefaction where a prominent, dark, branching, mosaic-like network of superficial veins becomes visible on the skin, resembling the patterns on green or reddish-purple marble.\n\nDuring decomposition, anaerobic bacteria in the bowel produce **hydrogen sulfide ($H_2S$)** gas. This gas diffuses through the tissues and reacts with the iron in the hemoglobin of disintegrating red blood cells within the superficial veins, forming **sulfhemoglobin**. This dark, greenish-black compound outlines the course of the cutaneous blood vessels.\n\n**Timeline:**\nTypically appears within **24 to 36 hours** after death (earlier in warm, humid weather) and is most prominent on the shoulders, chest, thighs, and abdomen.\n\n**Medico-Legal Importance (MLI):**\n* It is a sure sign of putrefaction.\n* It indicates that the postmortem interval is approximately 24 to 36 hours.\n* It must not be confused with ante-mortem bruising or pattern injuries.",
