    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Postmortem Changes & Artefacts",
    "answer": "### Adipocere (Saponification)\n\n**Definition and Mechanism:**\n**Adipocere** is a modification of putrefaction in which the body's fatty tissues are converted into a yellowish-white, waxy, greasy, firm substance with a sweetish, rancid odor. It is caused by the **hydrolysis and hydrogenation** of body fats. Unsaturated fatty acids (like oleic acid) are converted by bacterial enzymes (specifically from *Clostridium perfringens*) into saturated fatty acids (such as palmitic, stearic, and hydroxystearic acids).\n\n**Conditions Required:**\nRequires a **moist, damp, warm, and anaerobic environment** with restricted air circulation, commonly found in bodies submerged in water or buried in wet, clayey soil.\n\n**Medico-Legal Importance (MLI):**\n1. **Sure Sign of Death**: Confirms somatic and molecular death.\n2. **Preserves Features**: Retains the physical shape and facial profile, aiding in personal identification even after months.\n3. **Preserves Injuries**: Wounds like stabs, gunshot entries, and strangulation marks remain identifiable.\n4. **TSD and Environment**: Helps estimate the postmortem interval (takes weeks to months) and indicates the wet environment of burial.",
