    "id": 73,
    "text": "Entomology of Cadaver",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Postmortem Changes & Artefacts",
    "answer": "### Forensic Entomology of the Cadaver\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nForensic entomology is the study of insect biology, larval development, and ecological succession on a decomposing corpse to assist in legal investigations. Its primary objective is to estimate the **minimum Postmortem Interval (PMI)** or time since death (TSD).\n\n**Mechanism:**\n1. Within minutes of death, necrophagous insects, particularly blowflies (family *Calliphoridae*), are attracted by decomposition odors and lay eggs in natural orifices (eyes, nose, mouth) or wounds.\n2. The eggs hatch into larvae (maggots), which progress through three growth stages (instars) before pupating and emerging as adult flies.\n\n**Medico-Legal Importance (MLI):**\n* **PMI Estimation**: By analyzing the specific insect species, their stage of development (maggot length), and ambient temperature, pathologists can accurately calculate the TSD, especially after 48 hours when other methods fail.\n* **Entomotoxicology**: Analysis of maggots can detect drugs or poisons in cases where the body is too decomposed for routine toxicology.\n* **Body Movement**: A typical insect species on a corpse can reveal if the body was moved from its original environment.",
