    "id": 79,
    "text": "Features of mental Health care act 2017",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Psychiatry",
    "answer": "### Features of the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017\n\nThe **Mental Healthcare Act (MHCA), 2017** was enacted by the Parliament of India to align domestic laws with the United Nations Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (UNCRPD). It repealed the older Mental Health Act, 1987.\n\n**Key Features of the MHCA, 2017:**\n\n1. **Right to Mental Healthcare**:\n   * It guarantees every person the right to access high-quality, affordable mental healthcare and treatment from services run or funded by the government.\n2. **Decriminalization of Suicide (Section 115)**:\n   * Under Section 115, any person who attempts to commit suicide is presumed to be under severe stress, unless proven otherwise.\n   * Consequently, they cannot be prosecuted or punished under Section 309 of the Indian Penal Code. The government is mandated to provide care, treatment, and rehabilitation to such individuals.\n3. **Advance Directive**:\n   * It empowers individuals to write an \"Advance Directive\" stating how they wish to be treated (or not treated) and who should make decisions on their behalf (Nominated Representative) if they lose the capacity to make decisions in the future.\n4. **Nominated Representative (NR)**:\n   * Every person has the right to appoint a Nominated Representative who will assist them in making treatment decisions during periods of severe mental illness.\n5. **Mental Health Review Boards (MHRB)**:\n   * Quasi-judicial bodies established in every district to protect patient rights, register Advance Directives, and hear complaints regarding treatment.\n6. **Ban on Cruel Treatments**:\n   * Banned the use of unmodified Electroconvulsive Therapy (ECT). ECT must now only be administered under general anesthesia and muscle relaxants.\n   * Banned physical chaining and solitary confinement of mentally ill persons.\n7. **Confidentiality and Privacy**:\n   * Restricts the media from publishing photos or personal information of patients undergoing mental health treatment without their explicit consent.",
