    "id": 81,
    "text": "Differentiate Between True Insanity And Feigned Insanity.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Psychiatry",
    "answer": "### Medico-Legal Importance of Distinguishing Insanity\nIn criminal jurisprudence, distinguishing between genuine psychiatric illness (**True Insanity**) and simulated mental illness (**Feigned Insanity** / Malingering) is critical. Accused individuals often feign insanity to escape trial, sentencing, or criminal liability under **Section 84 IPC** (insanity defense).\n\n---\n\n### Comparison of True vs. Feigned Insanity\n\n| Clinical Feature | True Insanity | Feigned Insanity (Simulated) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Onset** | Usually gradual; preceded by prodromal changes in behavior and mood. | Sudden; typically occurs immediately after committing a crime or being arrested. |\n| **Symptom Consistency** | Symptoms align with established clinical syndromic patterns. | Symptoms are bizarre, exaggerated, and do not fit clinical classifications. |\n| **Observation (Watched vs. Unwatched)** | Behavior remains consistent whether the patient is observed or alone. | Behavior changes dramatically; patient behaves normally when they think they are alone. |\n| **Physical Signs & Exhaustion** | Features like insomnia, dry skin, coated tongue, and rapid pulse are common. | Physical parameters are completely normal; patient exhibits physical exhaustion from \"acting\". |\n| **Sleep Pattern** | Severe insomnia or highly disrupted sleep cycles are typical. | Sleep pattern is normal; the individual eventually sleeps soundly. |\n| **Response to Treatment** | Responds to antipsychotics or mood stabilizers; symptoms persist under placebo. | Does not respond to medication in a clinical manner; simulation breaks down under amobarbital. |\n| **Motive** | No obvious secondary gain; the illness existed prior to legal issues. | Clear, immediate motive to avoid legal punishment, imprisonment, or trial. |",
    "stars": 5
