    "id": 84,
    "text": "Mc Naughten Rule And Durhams Rule.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Psychiatry",
    "answer": "### McNaughten Rule and Durham's Rule\n\nThese are two historical legal benchmarks used to determine the criminal responsibility of an accused claiming the defense of insanity:\n\n**1. McNaughten Rule (1843):**\n* Formulated in England, it is the foundation of the insanity defense in English and Indian law (Sec 84 IPC).\n* It states that to establish a defense of insanity, it must be proved that at the time of the act, the accused was laboring under such a **defect of reason, from disease of the mind**, as not to know the nature and quality of the act, or if they did know it, that they did not know it was wrong.\n\n**2. Durham's Rule (1954):**\n* Known as the **\"product test\"** of insanity.\n* It states that an accused is not criminally responsible if their unlawful act was the **product of a mental disease or mental defect**.\n* It is much broader than McNaughten but was criticized for being too vague and relies heavily on medical testimony, leading to its eventual abandonment in most jurisdictions.",
