    "text": "Sec 84 IPC .",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Psychiatry",
    "answer": "### Section 84 of the Indian Penal Code (IPC)\n\n**Section 84 IPC** governs the criminal responsibility of a person of unsound mind in India. It states:\n> *\"Nothing is an offence which is done by a person who, at the time of doing it, by reason of unsoundness of mind, is incapable of knowing the nature of the act, or that he is doing what is either wrong or contrary to law.\"*\n\n**Key Points:**\n* **Legal Insanity**: This section recognizes **legal insanity** (cognitive impairment) rather than mere **medical insanity** (presence of a psychiatric illness).\n* **Timing**: The unsoundness of mind must be present at the **exact moment** the offense was committed.\n* **Burden of Proof**: Under Section 105 of the Indian Evidence Act, the burden of proving insanity lies entirely on the accused, who must establish it on a preponderance of probabilities.",
