    "text": "Automatism",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Psychiatry",
    "answer": "### Automatism\n\n**Definition:**\n**Automatism** is a state in which a person performs involuntary, unconscious, and mechanical physical acts without any conscious willpower, control, or subsequent memory of the event.\n\n**Types of Automatism:**\n1. **Insane Automatism**: Caused by an internal factor or disease of the mind (e.g., epilepsy, brain tumor, schizophrenia). Legally, this leads to a verdict of \"not guilty by reason of insanity\" and placement in a psychiatric facility.\n2. **Non-Insane Automatism**: Caused by an external factor (e.g., physical concussion from a head injury, somnambulism, hypoglycemia, night terrors, or involuntary drug intoxication). This leads to a complete acquittal in court.\n\n**Medico-Legal Importance (MLI):**\nAutomatism is a powerful defense because it negates the **mens rea** (guilty mind) and voluntary nature of the **actus reus** (physical act). It must be thoroughly evaluated by forensic experts to rule out malingering (feigning).",
