    "text": "Illusion",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Psychiatry",
    "answer": "### Illusion\n\n**Definition:**\nAn **illusion** is a false interpretation or misperception of a **real, external sensory stimulus**.\n\n**Examples:**\n* Mistaking a rope for a snake in the dark.\n* Hearing the rustling of dry leaves and misinterpreting it as human footsteps.\n* Seeing a shadow and believing it is a person.\n\n**Difference from Hallucination:**\nAn illusion always requires an actual, existing external stimulus to be misperceived. In contrast, a **hallucination** is a completely false sensory perception created by the mind in the absolute absence of any external physical stimulus.\n\n**Medico-Legal Importance (MLI):**\nIllusions are common in normal individuals under extreme fear, exhaustion, or poor lighting. However, they are also frequently observed in toxic or confusional states, such as high fever, delirium tremens, or organic brain syndromes, where they may lead to impulsive, self-defensive actions.",
