    "id": 88,
    "text": "Mens Rea And Actus Reus",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Psychiatry",
    "answer": "### Mens Rea and Actus Reus\n\nThese are the two fundamental, constituent elements required to establish criminal liability under criminal law, summarized by the Latin maxim:\n> *\"Actus non facit reum nisi mens sit rea\"* (An act does not make a person guilty unless their mind is also guilty).\n\n1. **Actus Reus (The Guilty Act)**:\n   * The physical act, conduct, or unlawful omission that constitutes a crime. The act must be voluntary and done under conscious control.\n2. **Mens Rea (The Guilty Mind)**:\n   * The mental element, criminal intent, recklessness, or knowledge of wrongdoing accompanying the physical act.\n\n**Medico-Legal Importance:**\nIn forensic psychiatry, conditions like legal insanity (Sec 84 IPC), sleepwalking (somnambulism), or non-insane automatism negate the *mens rea*. Because these conditions impair the cognitive capacity to form a voluntary intent, the accused cannot be held criminally responsible for their actions.",
