    "text": "What Is SOMNAMBULISM",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Psychiatry",
    "answer": "### Somnambulism (Sleepwalking)\n\n**Definition:**\n**Somnambulism** is a sleep disorder (parasomnia) characterized by complex, semi-purposeful behaviors—such as walking, dressing, or leaving the house—performed during deep, non-REM (N3) sleep. The individual is completely unconscious, lacks conscious control, and has no memory of the event upon waking.\n\n**Medico-Legal Importance (MLI):**\n1. **Non-Insane Automatism**: Acts committed during sleepwalking (including rare instances of homicidal violence or self-harm) are classified as non-insane automatism. Since there is no **mens rea** (guilty intent), the individual is not criminally responsible.\n2. **Vulnerability**: Sleepwalkers are prone to accidental injuries, such as falling from open windows or balconies.\n3. **Malingering**: A defense of somnambulism must be thoroughly investigated using overnight polysomnography and psychiatric history to rule out feigned sleepwalking to escape criminal prosecution.",
