    "text": "Add A Note On ABRATIONS & It's Types And Its Medico Legal Importance.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Traumatology - Mechanical Injuries",
    "answer": "### Definition of an Abrasion\nAn **abrasion** is a superficial injury involving only the outer layer of the skin (epidermis) without breaching the dermis, caused by friction or pressure against a rough surface.\n\n---\n\n### Types of Abrasions\n1.  **Scratch**: A linear abrasion caused by a pointed object (e.g., fingernail, pin) dragging across the skin. The head of the scratch is clean, while the tail shows a collection of epithelial flakes, indicating the direction of force.\n2.  **Graze (Friction/Sliding/Brush)**: The most common type, caused by sliding or dragging over a rough surface (e.g., road traffic accidents). It shows parallel linear grooves.\n3.  **Pressure (Crush/Friction) Abrasion**: Caused by sustained pressure with minimal sliding. Examples include ligature marks in hanging or strangulation, and teeth marks.\n4.  **Impact (Contact) Abrasion**: Caused by sudden perpendicular impact, printing the pattern of the object onto the skin (e.g., radiator grille pattern, tyre tread, or muzzle mark in firearm injuries).\n\n---\n\n### Ageing of an Abrasion\n*   **Fresh**: Bright red, moist, and tender.\n*   **12 to 24 Hours**: Covered by lymph and blood (reddish scab).\n*   **2 to 3 Days**: Reddish-brown scab.\n*   **4 to 7 Days**: Scab becomes dark brown/black, starts peeling at the margins.\n*   **7 to 10 Days**: Scab falls off, leaving a depigmented or hyperpigmented area.\n\n---\n\n### Medico-Legal Importance\n1.  **Site of Impact**: Shows the exact area of trauma.\n2.  **Direction of Force**: Indicated by the tags of epidermis at the end of the graze.\n3.  **Time of Injury (Age of Abrasion)**: Determined by the color and state of the scab.\n4.  **Manner of Death**: \n    *   Fingernail scratches on the neck suggest manual strangulation.\n    *   Scratches on the thighs or breasts suggest sexual assault.\n    *   Scratches around the mouth and nose suggest smothering.\n    *   A circular muzzle mark indicates a contact firearm discharge.",
