    "text": "Incised Looking Laceration",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Traumatology - Mechanical Injuries",
    "answer": "An **incised-looking laceration** (also known as a split laceration or pseudo-incised wound) is a type of lacerating wound that closely resembles a clean-cut incised wound.\n\n*   **Mechanism**: It occurs when blunt force is applied perpendicularly to the skin overlying a superficial bony prominence (such as the scalp, forehead, shin, or cheekbones). The skin is crushed directly against the bone, causing it to split linearly.\n*   **Distinguishing Features**:\n    *   Under a hand lens, it shows **tissue bridging** (fine nerves, blood vessels, and connective tissue fibers crossing the gap at the depth of the wound).\n    *   The edges, though seemingly clean, are slightly **abraded and bruised** under magnification.\n    *   The **hair bulbs** at the margins are crushed rather than cleanly cut.\n*   **Medico-Legal Importance**: It is crucial not to misidentify this as a sharp-weapon injury, as doing so would lead to incorrect conclusions about the weapon used (blunt vs. sharp).",
