    "chapter": "Forensic Traumatology - Mechanical Injuries",
    "answer": "**Hesitation cuts** (or tentative cuts) are multiple, superficial, parallel incised wounds seen around the main, deep, fatal wound in cases of suicide.\n\n*   **Locations**: They are commonly found on the wrists (over the radial artery), the neck (in cut-throat wounds), or the anterior chest wall over the precordium.\n*   **Mechanism and Significance**:\n    *   They represent the victim's trial attempts, indecision, or fear of pain before inflicting the final, deep, fatal cut.\n    *   They are usually shallow, involving only the skin and subcutaneous tissue.\n*   **Medico-Legal Importance**:\n    *   **Manner of Death**: Their presence is highly diagnostic of **suicide**.\n    *   **Exclusion of Homicide**: They are absent in homicidal wounds, where instead defense wounds are found on the hands or forearms.\n    *   They help confirm the self-inflicted nature of the injuries and rule out struggle or third-party involvement.",
