    "text": "Differences Between Suicidal And Homicidal Cut Throat Wounds",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Traumatology - Mechanical Injuries",
    "answer": "Cut-throat wounds can be suicidal or homicidal, and they are differentiated by specific features:\n\n| Feature | Suicidal Cut Throat | Homicidal Cut Throat |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Direction** | Begins high on the side opposite to the hand used, running obliquely down and across the neck. | Usually horizontal, transverse, or irregular, and often at a lower level on the neck. |\n| **Depth** | Starts shallow, deepens in the middle, and ends shallow (tentative tailing). | Deep throughout, often cutting through the trachea, esophagus, and vertebrae. |\n| **Hesitation Cuts** | Present (multiple, superficial, parallel cuts at the commencement). | Absent. |\n| **Defense Wounds** | Absent. | Present (on palms, fingers, or forearms). |\n| **Carotid Sheath** | Often spared as the larynx is pulled forward, or only one side is cut. | Frequently severed completely on one or both sides. |\n| **Cadaveric Spasm** | May be present, holding the weapon firmly in the victim's hand. | Absent.",
