    "id": 100,
    "text": "Fabricated Wound",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Traumatology - Mechanical Injuries",
    "answer": "**Fabricated wounds** (also known as fictitious, forged, or self-inflicted wounds) are injuries produced by a person on their own body, or with the help of a friendly associate.\n\n*   **Motive**: \n    *   To bring a false charge of assault against an enemy.\n    *   To turn a simple case into a serious one (e.g., charging homicide/grievous hurt).\n    *   To escape duty or gain sympathy.\n*   **Characteristics**:\n    *   **Nature**: Mostly superficial, small, multiple, and parallel.\n    *   **Sites**: Located in easily accessible parts of the body (e.g., anterior chest, abdomen, outer aspect of thighs/forearms), usually avoiding vital areas like the eyes, genitals, or heart.\n    *   **Pattern**: Regularly arranged, avoiding clothing (clothes are often intact or slashed separately).\n    *   **Manner**: Absence of defense wounds or hesitation cuts.\n*   **Medico-Legal Importance**: They expose fraudulent allegations and must be carefully distinguished from real assault wounds.",
