    "text": "Causes Of Black Eye.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Forensic Traumatology - Mechanical Injuries",
    "answer": "A **black eye** (periorbital hematoma or ecchymosis) is an effusion of blood into the loose subcutaneous tissues of the eyelids and periorbital area.\n\n*   **Causes**:\n    1.  **Direct Trauma**: A direct blow to the eye, orbit, or eyebrows (e.g., punch, kick, or fall).\n    2.  **Indirect Trauma (Migratory/Ectopic)**:\n        *   **Fracture of the Anterior Cranial Fossa**: Blood tracks forward from the skull base under the periosteum into the orbit (spectacle eyes/raccoon eyes). Usually bilateral, appearing delayed, and without damage to the overlying eyelid skin.\n        *   **Frontal Scalp Injury**: Blood tracks downward due to gravity across the forehead into the upper eyelids.\n        *   **Fracture of Nasal Bones**: Blood tracks laterally into the eyelids.\n*   **Medico-Legal Importance**: Distinguishing direct blow (shows local abrasion/bruise on eyelid) from skull fracture (no local skin injury, delayed bilateral appearance) is critical in head injury cases.",
