    "text": "PUPPE’s Rule",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Regional Injuries",
    "answer": "**Puppe's Rule** (or Puppe's Law) is a forensic principle used to determine the chronological order of multiple skull fractures when they intersect.\n\n*   **Principle**:\n    *   When a second fracture line propagates through the skull, it will **terminate/stop** when it reaches a pre-existing (first) fracture line. It cannot cross the gap of an already existing fracture because the stress/tension in the bone is dissipated at the pre-existing crack.\n*   **Application**:\n    *   If Fracture Line A is continuous and Fracture Line B stops at its intersection with Line A, then **Fracture A occurred first** and **Fracture B occurred second**.\n*   **Medico-Legal Importance**: It is invaluable in homicidal head injury cases to reconstruct the sequence of blows inflicted on the victim's head.",
