    "text": "Gunpowder n types",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Firearm & Explosion Injuries",
    "answer": "**Gunpowder** is the propellant used in ammunition that burns rapidly to produce gas, driving the projectile out of the firearm.\n\n*   **Types of Gunpowder**:\n    1.  **Black Powder (Traditional)**:\n        *   Composition: Potassium nitrate (75%), sulfur (10%), and charcoal (15%).\n        *   Features: Produces a large amount of smoke, flame, and abundant soot. Contact wounds show extensive blackening and tattooing.\n    2.  **Smokeless Powder (Modern)**:\n        *   Composition: Nitrocellulose (single-base) or nitrocellulose combined with nitroglycerin (double-base).\n        *   Features: Burns completely with very little residue and almost no smoke. It produces less soot and tattooing, which are usually light grey or yellow-green under magnification.\n*   **Medico-Legal Importance**: Identifying the powder type at autopsy (via chemical analysis of residues) helps determine the ammunition type, weapon type, and firing range.",
