    "id": 135,
    "text": "Write in detail about the definition, types of Hanging, and causes of death in Hanging. Describe the postmortem findings in a case of death due to hanging.",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "Mechanical Asphyxia",
    "answer": "### Definition of Hanging\n**Hanging** is a form of mechanical asphyxia in which the body is suspended by a ligature around the neck, and the constricting force is the weight of the body (either whole or part of it).\n\n---\n\n### Types of Hanging\nHanging is classified based on the degree of suspension and the position of the knot:\n\n#### 1. Based on the Degree of Suspension\n*   **Complete Hanging**: The body is fully suspended off the ground, and no part of it touches the floor. The entire body weight acts as the constricting force on the neck.\n*   **Partial Hanging**: Some part of the body (such as the feet, knees, buttocks, or toes) touches the ground. Only a fraction of the body weight acts as the constricting force. This is very common in suicidal hangings, as a weight of only 2 to 3 kg is sufficient to close the jugular veins and carotid arteries, leading to rapid unconsciousness and death.\n\n#### 2. Based on the Position of the Knot\n*   **Typical Hanging**: The knot of the ligature is placed in the midline of the back of the neck (occipital region). This leads to symmetrical stretching of the neck structures and maximum, immediate closure of both the airway and major blood vessels.\n*   **Atypical Hanging**: The knot is placed at any other position on the neck, such as the side of the neck (lateral), under the chin (submental), or under the jaw. This leads to asymmetrical compression.\n\n---\n\n### Causes of Death in Hanging\nDeath in hanging can occur due to one or a combination of the following physiological mechanisms:\n\n1.  **Asphyxia (Airway Obstruction)**:\n    *   The ligature forces the hyoid bone and the tongue upwards and backwards against the posterior pharyngeal wall. \n    *   This blocks the pharyngeal airway, while the epiglottis folds over the laryngeal inlet, cutting off oxygen.\n    *   *Pressure required*: About 15 kg.\n2.  **Venous Congestion (Jugular Vein Occlusion)**:\n    *   The thin-walled jugular veins are easily compressed by the ligature. This prevents venous blood from draining from the brain back to the heart, leading to rapid cerebral congestion, hypoxia, and immediate loss of consciousness.\n    *   *Pressure required*: Only 2 kg.\n3.  **Cerebral Ischemia (Carotid Artery Occlusion)**:\n    *   The carotid arteries are compressed, cutting off the supply of oxygenated blood to the brain. This results in immediate unconsciousness within 10 to 15 seconds.\n    *   *Pressure required*: 3.5 to 5 kg (Vertebral arteries require about 20 kg).\n4.  **Vagal Inhibition (Reflex Cardiac Arrest)**:\n    *   Pressure on the carotid sinuses or the vagus nerves in the neck triggers strong reflex stimulation of the parasympathetic nervous system, leading to instant cardiac arrest.\n5.  **Fracture-Dislocation of Cervical Vertebrae**:\n    *   Typically seen in **judicial hanging** (long drop hanging). The sudden drop and snap fracture the axis vertebra (specifically the odontoid process) or cause dislocation at the C2-C3 or C3-C4 level. This crushes the spinal cord and brainstem, causing instant death.\n\n---\n\n### External Postmortem Findings\n\n#### 1. Ligature Mark\nThe ligature mark is the most important diagnostic feature of hanging. Its characteristics include:\n*   **Location**: Situated high up in the neck, above the thyroid cartilage, between the hyoid bone and the chin.\n*   **Direction**: Oblique and non-continuous. It runs upwards towards the site of the knot, where there is usually a gap or a fading of the mark.\n*   **Appearance**: A dry, hard, pale-yellowish, or brown parchment-like groove with congested and slightly abraded margins.\n\n#### 2. Face and Head\n*   **Pale Face**: In complete, typical hanging, the face is pale and placid because the carotid arteries are closed immediately.\n*   **Congested Face**: In partial or atypical hanging, the face may be congested, cyanosed, and bloated, with prominent petechial hemorrhages (**Tardieu spots**) on the conjunctivae and forehead. The eyes may be partially open, with dilated pupils. A bloody discharge from the nose or mouth is rare but may occur.\n*   **Tongue**: Protruded, swollen, and bitten, often dry and dark-brown at the tip.\n*   **Saliva Trickling**: A trickle of saliva from the corner of the mouth opposite to the knot. This is a **highly reliable antemortem sign** of hanging, caused by pressure on the salivary glands during life.\n\n#### 3. Postmortem Lividity\n*   Located in the lower extremities (feet, legs) and hands (glove and stocking distribution) in suspended bodies, showing petechiae due to capillary rupture under gravity.\n\n#### 4. Genitals\n*   Seminal fluid may be present at the urethral meatus, and the penis may appear semi-erect (due to hypostatic pooling of blood).\n\n---\n\n### Internal Postmortem Findings\n\n#### 1. Neck Dissection\n*   **Subcutaneous Tissue**: White, glistening, and dry under the ligature mark.\n*   **Muscles**: Hemorrhages or tears in the sternocleidomastoid, platysma, and thyrohyoid muscles.\n*   **Hyoid Bone and Laryngeal Cartilages**: Fractures are rare in young individuals but common in elderly victims. The **hyoid bone fracture** in hanging is usually an **inward compression fracture** (unlike strangulation where it is outward).\n*   **Carotid Arteries**: Tear of the inner coat (intima) of the common carotid artery (**Amussat's sign**), showing transverse tears just below the bifurcation.\n*   **Histopathology of Ligature Mark**: Slicing the skin under the mark for histopathological examination reveals micro-hemorrhages, collagen fiber condensation, and vital cellular infiltration, proving the antemortem nature of the injury.\n\n#### 2. Respiratory and Cerebral Findings\n*   The larynx and trachea are highly congested and contain frothy mucus.\n*   Lungs are pale or congested with subpleural Tardieu spots.\n*   The brain shows general congestion, edema, and petechial hemorrhages in the white matter.\n\n#### 3. Abdominal Visera\n*   Congested. **Wischnewsky’s spots** (gastric mucosal hemorrhages) are absent unless cold was a factor, but general asphyxial congestion is present.",
