    "text": "Add A Note On CAFÉ CORONARY SYNDROME.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Mechanical Asphyxia",
    "answer": "### Definition and Concept\n**Cafe Coronary Syndrome** is a form of accidental asphyxiation resulting from complete acute obstruction of the upper respiratory tract (specifically the larynx or trachea) by a large bolus of poorly chewed food, most commonly a chunk of meat. \n\nThe name \"Cafe Coronary\" is derived from the fact that this fatal incident typically occurs in restaurants or during meals, and is frequently misdiagnosed as an acute myocardial infarction (coronary occlusion) because of the sudden, dramatic collapse of the victim.\n\n---\n\n### Pathophysiology and Clinical Presentation\nDuring a meal, a large food bolus fails to be adequately chewed and is swallowed whole. It becomes impacted at the level of the epiglottis, larynx, or upper trachea. This triggers:\n1.  **Immediate Airway Obstruction:** The victim is suddenly unable to breathe, speak, or cough.\n2.  **The \"Heimlich Sign\":** Involuntarily, the victim clutches their throat with their hand, signaling airway distress.\n3.  **Reflex Vagal Inhibition:** Mechanical stimulation of the sensitive laryngeal nerves can trigger instantaneous cardiac arrest.\n4.  **Cyanosis and Collapse:** Within seconds, the victim turns blue (cyanosed), loses consciousness, and collapses, mimicking a sudden heart attack.\n\n---\n\n### Predisposing Factors\n*   **Alcohol Intoxication:** Depresses the protective laryngeal reflexes and impairs judgment.\n*   **Poor Dentition or Dentures:** Prevents proper chewing of food.\n*   **Neurological Disorders:** Parkinson's disease, stroke, or senile dementia which impair the swallowing reflex.\n*   **Gluttony:** Fast, hurried eating or talking while chewing.\n\n---\n\n### Management\nThe definitive emergency management is the **Heimlich Maneuver** (subdiaphragmatic abdominal thrusts) to force the diaphragm upward, generating enough air pressure to expel the foreign body. If unsuccessful, emergency cricothyroidotomy or tracheostomy is required.\n\n---\n\n### Postmortem Findings\nAt autopsy, the diagnosis is confirmed by finding a large, unchewed bolus of food impacted tightly in the larynx or trachea, with signs of acute asphyxiation and absence of significant coronary artery occlusion.",
