    "text": "Write About Various Types Of SUFFOCATION",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Mechanical Asphyxia",
    "answer": "### Definition\n**Suffocation** is a broad category of mechanical asphyxia caused by the deprivation of oxygen due to an external cause other than direct pressure on the neck (such as hanging or strangulation). It occurs when there is a physical barrier preventing the entry of air into the respiratory system.\n\n---\n\n### Types of Suffocation\n\n#### 1. Smothering\nSmothering is caused by the mechanical closure of the external respiratory orifices—the mouth and nostrils—by a physical barrier. This can be accomplished by hands (forcible smothering), pillows, cushions, adhesive tape, or by pressing the face against bedclothes or soft soil. It is commonly homicidal in infants or the elderly, and accidental in infants sleeping with parents (overlaying).\n\n#### 2. Choking\nChoking is the mechanical obstruction of the internal air passages (pharynx, larynx, or trachea) by a foreign object. This object can be:\n*   **Intrinsic/Endogenous:** Aspirated blood, vomitus, or anatomical structures (like a retroverted tongue in an unconscious patient).\n*   **Extrinsic/Exogenous:** A bolus of food (as in Cafe Coronary), coins, marbles, seeds, or toys.\n\n#### 3. Traumatic Asphyxia (Crush/Compressive Asphyxia)\nThis occurs when the external chest wall and abdomen are subjected to intense mechanical compression, preventing the respiratory movements of the chest and diaphragm. Air cannot be drawn into the lungs. It is typically accidental, occurring in stampedes, mine collapses, or vehicle rollovers.\n\n#### 4. Environmental Suffocation (Suffocation in Confined Space)\nThis results from being trapped in an airtight, enclosed space (such as a refrigerator, safe, or submarine) where the available oxygen is progressively depleted and carbon dioxide accumulates. It can also occur in deep wells, sewers, or silos where toxic gases displace oxygen.\n\n---\n\n### Medicolegal Significance\nAutopsy findings in smothering are often minimal, especially if a soft object was used, requiring careful scene investigation. Choking is diagnosed by retrieving the foreign body, and traumatic asphyxia is characterized by the classic *masque ecchymotique*.",
