    "id": 140,
    "text": "Write About TRAUMATIC ASPHYXIAAnd Postmortem Appearances.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Mechanical Asphyxia",
    "answer": "### Concept and Mechanism\n**Traumatic Asphyxia** (also known as crush or compressive asphyxia) is a form of mechanical asphyxia caused by severe, sustained compression of the chest and abdomen by a heavy weight, which physically prevents the respiratory movements of the thorax and diaphragm.\n\n#### The Haemodynamic Mechanism:\nWhen the chest is compressed, the respiratory muscles are locked. Concurrently, the intense pressure forces blood out of the right atrium back into the large veins of the head and neck. Because the superior vena cava and the veins of the head and neck lack valves, this retrograde pressure is transmitted directly to the capillaries. This causes extreme venous engorgement, capillary dilatation, and widespread capillary rupture in the head, neck, and upper thorax.\n\n---\n\n### Postmortem Appearances\n\n#### 1. External Appearances\n*   **Masque Ecchymotique (Ecchymotic Mask):** This is the hallmark sign. It is a deep purple, blue, or violet discoloration of the skin of the face, neck, and upper chest. The discoloration stops abruptly at the level of the clavicles or where tight clothing (like a collar) restricted capillary distension.\n*   **Conjunctival and Scleral Hemorrhages:** The eyes show massive subconjunctival hemorrhages, often making the sclera look completely red.\n*   **Petechial Hemorrhages:** Thousands of pinpoint petechial hemorrhages are visible on the face, eyelids, forehead, and oral mucosa.\n*   **Bleeding:** Epistaxis (bleeding from the nose) and bleeding from the ears may be present due to capillary rupture.\n\n#### 2. Internal Appearances\n*   **Visceral Congestion:** Massive congestion of the brain, meninges, lungs, and liver.\n*   **Tardieu Spots:** Plentiful petechial hemorrhages on the pleura, pericardium, and thymus.\n*   **Associated Skeletal Trauma:** Fractures of the ribs, clavicles, or sternum are common, along with lacerations of the lungs or liver.\n\n---\n\n### Medicolegal Significance\nTraumatic asphyxia is almost always **accidental**. It is classically seen in crowd stampedes, industrial accidents, cave-ins, and building collapses (e.g., earthquakes).",
