    "id": 141,
    "text": "Postmortem Findings In A Case Of Typical Drowning",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Mechanical Asphyxia",
    "answer": "### Introduction\nAt autopsy, the diagnosis of a typical (wet) drowning relies on a combination of characteristic external and internal findings. These findings are produced by active water inhalation and respiratory struggle.\n\n---\n\n### External Findings\n1.  **Froth at the Mouth and Nostrils:** This is the most diagnostic external sign. It is a fine, white, persistent, lathery, and odorless foam. It is formed by the mixing of inhaled water, mucus, and air by violent respiratory movements. It does not disappear easily and re-emerges if the chest is squeezed.\n2.  **Cadaveric Spasm:** If the victim, while drowning, grasped weeds, sand, mud, or gravel from the water bed, and these are found tightly gripped in the hand, it is **absolute proof** of antemortem drowning.\n3.  ** Washerwoman's Hands and Feet:** The skin of the palms and soles is wrinkled, pale, and sodden due to prolonged exposure to water. This is an indicator of immersion, not necessarily drowning itself.\n4.  **Hypostasis:** Postmortem lividity is often pale, pinkish, or bright red due to the oxygenation of blood by cold water. It is typically found on the face, neck, and front of the chest, as drowning victims usually float face down.\n\n---\n\n### Internal Findings\n1.  **Froth in the Air Passages:** Fine, white froth is found filling the larynx, trachea, and bronchi.\n2.  **Emphysema Aquosum:** The lungs are highly voluminous, heavy, boggy, and pale. They completely fill the thoracic cavity and meet in the midline. When removed, they do not collapse, and they pit on pressure. Crepitus is lost.\n3.  **Paltauf's Hemorrhages:** These are faint, shiny, reddish-blue subpleural ecchymotic patches, measuring 1 to 2 cm, found in the lower lobes of the lungs, caused by the rupture of alveolar walls.\n4.  **Foreign Matter in stomach:** Water, mud, sand, or aquatic plants are found in the stomach and duodenum, swallowed during the active struggle.\n5.  **Diatoms:** The recovery of microscopic diatoms from closed organs like the bone marrow confirms antemortem drowning.",
