    "id": 144,
    "text": "Write About HYOID BONE FRACTURES & It's Types.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Mechanical Asphyxia",
    "answer": "### Anatomy and Fusion\nThe hyoid bone is a U-shaped bone situated in the anterior midline of the neck between the mandible and the thyroid cartilage. It consists of a central body and two greater and lesser cornua. \n\nIn young individuals, the greater cornua are joined to the body by flexible cartilage. As age advances, typically after 30 to 40 years, this cartilage undergoes fusion (ankylosis) and becomes calcified. Consequently, the hyoid bone becomes brittle and highly susceptible to fractures in older individuals, while remaining highly resistant in children and young adults.\n\n---\n\n### Types of Hyoid Bone Fractures\n\n#### 1. Inward Compression Fracture\n*   **Mechanism:** Caused by external force compressing the greater cornua inward toward the midline.\n*   **Features:** The fracture occurs at the junction of the posterior third and anterior two-thirds of the greater cornua, with the broken fragments displaced inward.\n*   **Occurrence:** Highly characteristic of **manual strangulation (throttling)**, where the fingers squeeze the neck.\n\n#### 2. Outward Traction (Tension) Fracture\n*   **Mechanism:** Caused by lateral pull or traction on the greater cornua.\n*   **Features:** The fragments are displaced outward and backward.\n*   **Occurrence:** Seen in **hanging**, due to the upward and outward pull exerted by the ligature loop.\n\n#### 3. Direct Anteroposterior Compression Fracture\n*   **Mechanism:** Caused by direct impact from the front.\n*   **Occurrence:** Seen in road traffic accidents (steering wheel injuries) or direct blows to the neck.\n\n---\n\n### Medicolegal Significance\n*   **Differentiating Hanging from Strangulation:** Finding an inward fracture of the hyoid is strong evidence of manual strangulation (homicide). In suicidal hanging, hyoid fractures are rare (under 15% of cases), except in elderly individuals.\n*   **Vital Reaction:** The presence of localized hemorrhage around the fracture site confirms that the injury was sustained **antemortem**.",
