    "id": 145,
    "text": "Write About DIATOMS And Its MLI.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Mechanical Asphyxia",
    "answer": "### Definition\n**Diatoms** are microscopic, unicellular, photosynthetic algae belonging to the class Bacillariophyceae. They are characterized by a unique, rigid, and highly protective cell wall made of silica, known as a **frustule**. This siliceous wall is extremely resistant to heat, strong acids, and biological putrefaction.\n\n---\n\n### The Diatom Test: Physiological Basis\nWhen a living person drowns, they actively inhale water containing diatoms. \n1.  These microscopic algae reach the alveoli, where the pressure of struggling causes them to rupture the alveolar membrane.\n2.  The diatoms enter the pulmonary capillaries, join the active systemic circulation, and are distributed to distant, closed organs that have no direct contact with the external medium. These include the **femur/sternum bone marrow**, brain, liver, kidneys, and spleen.\n3.  If a dead body is thrown into water (postmortem immersion), there is no active circulation. Although water may passively seep into the lungs, diatoms cannot cross the alveolar barrier and travel to the bone marrow or brain.\n\n---\n\n### Chemical Procedure\nThe gold standard is the **acid digestion method**. A sample of bone marrow (usually from the femur) or solid organ tissue is boiled with concentrated nitric acid to destroy all organic matter. The acid-resistant siliceous diatoms survive. The residue is centrifuged and examined under a microscope.\n\n---\n\n### Medicolegal Importance (MLI)\n1.  **Diagnosing Antemortem Drowning:** The recovery of diatoms from closed organs (especially the bone marrow) is considered **conclusive proof** of antemortem drowning.\n2.  **Pinpointing the Location of Drowning:** Matching the specific species of diatoms recovered from the bone marrow with the diatom profile of the water body where the body was found confirms the exact site of drowning.\n3.  **Detecting Disposal:** If the diatom species in the bone marrow do not match the water where the body was found, it suggests the victim drowned elsewhere and was postmortemly disposed of.",
    "stars": 4
