    "id": 146,
    "text": "Signs Of Ante-Mortem Hanging",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Mechanical Asphyxia",
    "answer": "### Signs of Ante-Mortem Hanging\n\nTo determine if a hanging was antemortem (occurred during life), a forensic pathologist relies on several key vital signs:\n\n1.  **Dried Saliva Trickle:** A stream of dried saliva running down from the corner of the mouth, opposite to the position of the knot. This is a pathognomonic vital sign, as saliva secretion requires active salivary gland function under life pressure.\n2.  **Ligature Mark Features:** The skin beneath the ligature mark is dry, hard, and parchment-like (yellowish-brown). Microscopically, it shows cellular infiltration (polymorphonuclear leukocytes) and tissue congestion.\n3.  **Amussat's Sign:** Transverse tearing of the inner coat (intima and media) of the common carotid arteries, accompanied by localized extravasation of blood.\n4.  **Congestion and Petechiae:** Widespread petechial hemorrhages (Tardieu spots) restricted to the skin, conjunctiva, and eyelids above the level of the ligature mark.\n5.  **Periligature Injuries:** Hemorrhages in the subcutaneous tissues and neck muscles beneath the mark.",
