    "text": "Tardieu Spots",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Mechanical Asphyxia",
    "answer": "### Tardieu Spots\n\n**Tardieu spots** are small, pinpoint, dark-red petechial hemorrhages (extravasations of blood) visible under the visceral pleura of the lungs, the epicardium of the heart, the conjunctivae, and the skin of the face. First described in detail by the French forensic pioneer Ambroise Tardieu, they are caused by the acute rise in intravascular pressure inside capillaries. \n\nWhen a person undergoes mechanical asphyxiation, high intrathoracic pressure and hypoxic damage to capillary walls lead to their dilatation and rupture. Although historically considered pathognomonic of mechanical asphyxia (especially smothering), they are now known to be **non-specific**. They can appear in any rapid death (such as coronary failure, electrocution, or poisoning) and can also form as a postmortem artifact in dependent parts of the body due to gravity (hypostasis). Thus, they must be interpreted alongside other autopsy findings.",
