    "text": "Lynching",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Mechanical Asphyxia",
    "answer": "### Lynching\n\n**Lynching** is a form of extrajudicial public execution carried out by an informal group or mob, without any legal trial or authority, to punish an alleged offender or to intimidate a specific community. Historically and forensically, it is classified as a form of **homicidal hanging**.\n\n#### Medicolegal Significance:\nDuring the autopsy of a lynching victim, the forensic pathologist must document:\n1.  **Dual Patterns of Injury:** The body will display classic signs of homicidal hanging (such as a transverse ligature mark, thyroid cartilage fractures, and asphyxial signs) combined with extensive blunt force or sharp force injuries.\n2.  **Ante-mortem Trauma:** Multiple contusions, lacerations, defense wounds on the forearms, or fractures inflicted by the mob before or during the suspension.\n3.  **Cause of Death:** Usually asphyxia from hanging or shock/hemorrhage from mob-inflicted violence. Forensic evidence is vital to establish homicidal intent and prosecute the perpetrators of mob violence.",
