    "text": "Garroting                                    B) Bansdola",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Mechanical Asphyxia",
    "answer": "### A) Garroting\nGarroting is a method of homicidal strangulation where the victim's neck is suddenly constricted using a cord, wire, rope, or collar from behind. The loop is tightened either by pulling the ends or by twisting a stick inserted into the ligature (Spanish windlass). Death occurs rapidly due to asphyxia or bilateral carotid occlusion. Autopsy shows a prominent, horizontal, low-lying ligature mark completely encircling the neck, with extensive deep muscle bruising and frequent intimal tearing of the carotid arteries.\n\n---\n\n### B) Bansdola\nBansdola is a highly lethal form of homicidal strangulation occasionally encountered in India. The victim's neck is compressed between two strong, parallel wooden logs or bamboo sticks, which are placed in front and behind the throat and tied tightly together at both ends. \n\nAutopsy findings are characteristic: severe crushing of the cervical tissues, extensive lacerations of the neck muscles, fractured hyoid bone, thyroid cartilage, and trachea, accompanied by massive localized bruising and extravasation of blood.",
