    "text": "Paltaufs Hemorrhage",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Mechanical Asphyxia",
    "answer": "### Paltauf's Hemorrhage\n\n**Paltauf's hemorrhages** (or Paltauf's spots) are faint, shiny, reddish-blue or yellowish-brown ecchymotic patches, measuring up to 1 to 2 cm, found under the visceral pleura of the lungs, especially in the lower lobes. \n\n#### Pathophysiology:\nDuring the active phases of drowning, the victim makes violent, forced inspiratory efforts against water. This generates extreme pressure inside the alveoli, stretching and rupturing the alveolar walls and their associated capillaries. The resulting blood extravasates into the subpleural space, forming these characteristic spots.\n\n#### Medicolegal Significance:\nPaltauf's hemorrhages are a critical vital sign of **antemortem typical (wet) drowning**. Their presence helps the forensic pathologist distinguish a case of actual drowning from a situation where a dead body was thrown into water to conceal a homicide (postmortem submersion), where these spots will be absent.",
