    "answer": "### Masque Ecchymotique\n\n**Masque ecchymotique** (also known as the \"ecchymotic mask\" or traumatic asphyxia) is a striking postmortem appearance characterized by deep blue, purple, or black cyanosis of the face, neck, and upper chest, accompanied by thousands of pinpoint petechial hemorrhages in the skin, eyelids, and conjunctivae.\n\n#### Pathophysiology:\nIt is caused by severe, sustained mechanical compression of the chest and abdomen (such as in landslide collapses or stampedes). This prevents respiratory movements and forces venous blood out of the right atrium into the superior vena cava. Because the veins of the head and neck lack valves, this intense retrograde pressure causes massive capillary engorgement and widespread capillary rupture.\n\n#### Medicolegal Significance:\nIt is highly diagnostic of **traumatic or crush asphyxia**. The discoloration stops abruptly at the level of collar pressure or clavicles, indicating the zone of compression, and is almost always accidental.",
