    "id": 153,
    "text": "LA FACIE SYMPATHIQUE",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Mechanical Asphyxia",
    "answer": "### La Facie Sympathique\n\n***La facie sympathique*** (the sympathetic face) is a classical, albeit rare, ocular sign observed during the postmortem examination of a victim of hanging. \n\n#### Physical Appearance:\nIt is characterized by an asymmetric facial expression where one eye remains open with a dilated pupil (mydriasis) and a widened palpebral fissure, while the other eye is closed or shows a normal/constricted pupil.\n\n#### Mechanism:\nThis phenomenon is caused by the asymmetric mechanical irritation or compression of the cervical sympathetic chain (sympathetic trunk) by the ligature loop on one side of the neck during the process of hanging. The irritation triggers unilateral sympathetic nervous stimulation, leading to pupillary dilation and eyelid retraction.\n\n#### Medicolegal Significance:\nIt is a strong vital sign indicating **antemortem hanging**. Since pupillary dilation and nervous responses require viable, living tissue at the time of suspension, it helps exclude postmortem hanging of a dead body.",
