    "id": 156,
    "text": "Define IMPOTENCE AND STERILITY. Explain the causes of impotence and sterility in males .",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Sexual Jurisprudence",
    "answer": "### Definitions\n*   **Impotence:** The physical inability of an individual to perform the act of sexual intercourse (copulation).\n*   **Sterility:** The biological inability to procreate or fertilize the female ovum due to the absence of viable, functional spermatozoa. \n*   *Note:* An impotent man may be fertile (able to produce viable sperm), and a sterile man may be fully potent (able to achieve erection and copulate).\n\n---\n\n### Causes of Impotence in Males\n\n#### 1. Organic/Physical Causes\n*   **Congenital Anomalies:** Microphallus, congenital absence of the penis, double penis, or severe hypospadias/epispadias.\n*   **Acquired/Traumatic:** Penile amputation, pelvic fractures damaging the cavernous nerves, or spinal cord injuries causing erectile dysfunction.\n*   **Endocrine Disorders:** Hypogonadism, diabetes mellitus, hyperprolactinemia, and thyroid disorders.\n*   **Vascular Diseases:** Atherosclerosis of the pudendal arteries, preventing sufficient blood flow to the corpora cavernosa.\n*   **Systemic Diseases & Drugs:** Chronic renal failure, hepatic cirrhosis, antihypertensives (e.g., beta-blockers), antidepressants, alcohol, and drug abuse.\n\n#### 2. Psychological/Psychogenic Causes\nThese account for a large percentage of cases. They include performance anxiety, depression, severe stress, marital discord, religious guilt, or past sexual trauma.\n\n---\n\n### Causes of Sterility in Males\n\n#### 1. Testicular Factors (Azoospermia/Oligozoospermia)\n*   Congenital conditions such as cryptorchidism (undescended testes) or Klinefelter syndrome (47, XXY).\n*   Testicular atrophy due to bilateral mumps orchitis, tuberculosis, or high-dose radiation exposure.\n\n#### 2. Obstructive Factors\nBilateral blockage of the vas deferens or epididymis resulting from infections (gonorrhea, chlamydia) or voluntary bilateral vasectomy.\n\n---\n\n### Medicolegal Significance\nImpotence and sterility are key grounds for the nullity of marriage or divorce under various personal laws, and are crucial in cases of disputed paternity or allegations of rape.",
