    "text": "Signs of virginity.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Sexual Jurisprudence",
    "answer": "### Forensic Examination of Virginity\nIn forensic medicine, particularly in cases of nullity of marriage, rape allegations, or defamation suits, a medical officer may be asked to examine a female to determine her virginity. A **virgin** is defined as a female who has never experienced sexual intercourse.\n\n---\n\n### Physical Signs of Virginity\n\nThe diagnosis is based on a constellation of anatomical findings:\n\n1. **The Hymen**:\n   * *Status*: Intact, elastic, and functional. The hymenal opening is typically small (admitting only the tip of the index finger with difficulty).\n   * *Tears*: Absence of old, healed hymenal tears (which appear as carunculae myrtiformes).\n2. **The Vagina**:\n   * *Status*: Narrow, tight, and highly rugose. The vaginal walls are closely apposed.\n3. **The Vulva & Fourchette**:\n   * *Fourchette*: Intact, showing no signs of tearing or old scarring.\n   * *Posterior Commissure*: Uninjured and structurally intact.\n4. **Labia Minora & Majora**:\n   * *Status*: Plump, firm, and fully covering the vaginal introitus.\n5. **Breasts**:\n   * *Status*: Firm, hemispherical, and elastic, with small, unpigmented aureolae (highly variable and of low diagnostic value).",
