    "id": 162,
    "text": "Differences between true virgin and false virgin.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Sexual Jurisprudence",
    "answer": "### Concepts of Virginity in Forensic Medicine\nWhile a \"true\" virgin has clear, classic anatomical findings, anatomical variations can lead to a condition known as a \"false\" virgin. Distinguishing between them is highly crucial during court testimonies.\n\n---\n\n### Comparison of True and False Virgin\n\n| Feature | True Virgin | False Virgin |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Anatomical Definition** | A female who has never had sexual intercourse and possesses all physical signs of virginity (intact hymen, tight vagina, etc.). | A female who has had sexual intercourse but retains an intact, undamaged hymen. |\n| **State of Hymen** | The hymen is thin, typical, intact, and tears during the first coitus, causing bleeding. | The hymen is anatomically intact but possesses unusual characteristics (highly elastic, distensible, or thick). |\n| **Vaginal Tightness** | Vaginal rugae are highly intact, and walls are tight and closely apposed. | The vagina may show laxity or distension if repeated intercourse has occurred. |\n| **Coital Tear / Bleeding** | First intercourse leads to rupture of the hymen, accompanied by localized pain and fresh bleeding. | Intercourse occurs without causing tears or bleeding because the elastic hymen stretches to accommodate the penis and retracts afterward. |\n| **Medical / Surgical Alteration** | Hymen is natural and unaltered. | The hymen may have been surgically reconstructed via **Hymenoplasty** (restoration of the hymen to simulate virginity). |\n| **Evidentiary Weight** | Standard medical signs are fully present, supporting the legal presumption of virginity. | The physical appearance is deceptive; an intact hymen does not conclusively prove the absence of sexual intercourse. |",
