    "text": "Pseudocyesis.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Sexual Jurisprudence",
    "answer": "### Pseudocyesis\n\n**Pseudocyesis** (also known as false, phantom, or spurious pregnancy) is a psychosomatic condition in which a non-pregnant woman firmly believes she is pregnant and develops physical signs of pregnancy.\n\n---\n\n### Etiology and Features:\nIt is driven by an intense emotional desire to have a child or an extreme fear of pregnancy, causing endocrine alterations (increased prolactin and luteinizing hormone). \n\nThe patient exhibits:\n*   Amenorrhea (cessation of menstruation).\n*   Abdominal enlargement (due to gaseous distension or fat deposition).\n*   Breast enlargement and colostrum secretion.\n*   Subjective sensations of fetal movements (caused by intestinal peristalsis).\n\n---\n\n### Medicolegal Significance:\nPseudocyesis can be used in civil fraud to blackmail a partner, support false inheritance claims, or as a defense in criminal cases (e.g., to postpone a sentence). It is easily diagnosed by negative immunologic pregnancy tests and an ultrasound showing an empty uterus.",
