    "text": "Lochiaand its types.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Sexual Jurisprudence",
    "answer": "### Lochia and Its Types\n\n**Lochia** is the vaginal discharge occurring during the puerperium (the post-delivery period, lasting about 3 to 6 weeks). It consists of blood, tissue debris, decidual shreds, and mucus from the healing uterine cavity.\n\n---\n\n### Types and Timeline:\n1.  **Lochia Rubra (Red Lochia):** Occurs during the first 1 to 4 days post-delivery. It is bright red and consists mainly of blood, decidual shreds, and fetal membrane remnants.\n2.  **Lochia Serosa (Pink/Pale Lochia):** Occurs from the 5th to 9th day. It is yellowish-brown or pink, containing serum, leucocytes, and mucus.\n3.  **Lochia Alba (White Lochia):** Occurs from the 10th to 14th day (can last up to 3 weeks). It is creamy white or pale yellow, rich in decidual cells, leucocytes, mucus, and epithelial cells.\n\n---\n\n### Medicolegal Significance:\nEvaluating the presence and type of lochia is crucial for diagnosing a recent delivery in both living and deceased females, and helps estimate the time elapsed since childbirth.",
