    "id": 170,
    "text": "Posthumous child and its Medicolegal aspects.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Sexual Jurisprudence",
    "answer": "### Posthumous Child and Its Medicolegal Aspects\n\nA **posthumous child** is a child born after the death of their biological father.\n\n---\n\n### Medicolegal Aspects:\n\n#### 1. Legitimacy\nUnder Section 112 of the Indian Evidence Act, a child born within **280 days** after the dissolution of marriage (which includes dissolution by the husband's death), the mother remaining unmarried, is legally presumed to be the legitimate child of the deceased husband.\n\n#### 2. Property and Inheritance Rights\nThe law recognizes the rights of a child *in utero* (in the womb). A posthumous child enjoys full inheritance rights over the deceased father's estate, identical to a child born during the father's lifetime.\n\n#### 3. Paternity Disputes\nIf the child is born beyond the 280-day limit, legitimacy is contested. In such cases, forensic DNA profiling using preserved tissue samples of the deceased father or close relatives is essential to settle inheritance claims.",
