    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Sexual Jurisprudence",
    "answer": "### Tests for Impotency\n\nTo evaluate allegations of male impotence, the following clinical and objective tests are performed:\n\n1.  **Nocturnal Penile Tumescence (NPT) Test:** Uses a Rigiscan device to record spontaneous erections during REM sleep. Normal erections indicate psychogenic impotence, whereas their absence indicates organic impotence.\n2.  **Intracorporeal Injection (ICI) Test:** Injecting a vasoactive drug (e.g., papaverine or prostaglandin E1) into the corpus cavernosum. A rapid, rigid erection indicates normal vascular and erectile capability.\n3.  **Duplex Doppler Ultrasound:** Assesses penile arterial blood flow and venous occlusion to rule out vasculogenic erectile dysfunction.\n4.  **Hormonal Assay:** Measuring serum testosterone, prolactin, LH, and FSH levels to detect endocrine causes.\n5.  **Biophysiologic Tests:** Measuring bulbocavernosus reflex latency to evaluate neuropathic impotence.\n6.  **Psychological Evaluation:** In-depth interview to identify psychogenic stressors.",
