    "text": "Define RAPE ( section 375 IPC)                                       (3+7+5) b)Describe in detail about the procedure of examination of a VICTIM OF  RAPE. c) Add a note on materials to be collected for laboratory investigations.",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "Sexual Offences",
    "answer": "### a) Definition of Rape (Section 375 IPC)\nUnder Section 375 of the Indian Penal Code (amended by the Criminal Law Amendment Acts of 2013 and 2018), rape is defined as any of the following acts committed by a man on a woman:\n1.  Penetrating his penis, to any extent, into the vagina, mouth, urethra, or anus of a woman.\n2.  Inserting, to any extent, any object or part of the body, not being the penis, into the vagina, urethra, or anus of a woman.\n3.  Manipulating any part of the body of a woman so as to cause penetration into the vagina, urethra, or anus.\n4.  Applying his mouth to the vagina, anus, or urethra of a woman.\n\n**Vitiating Circumstances (Without Consent):**\nThese acts constitute rape if committed:\n*   Against her will or without her consent.\n*   With her consent, when obtained by putting her in fear of death or hurt.\n*   With her consent, when the man knows he is not her husband, and she consents under the belief that he is.\n*   With her consent, when she is intoxicated, unsound of mind, or unable to understand the nature of the act.\n*   With or without her consent, when she is under **18 years of age** (Statutory Rape).\n\n---\n\n### b) Procedure of Examination of a Victim of Rape\n\nThe examination must be conducted by a registered medical practitioner (preferably female) with utmost dignity and care:\n\n#### 1. Consent and Preliminaries\nStrict, informed written consent is mandatory. If the victim is under 18, consent must be obtained from a parent or guardian. The date, time, and place of examination, along with two identification marks, must be recorded.\n\n#### 2. Detailed History\nRecord the history in the victim's own words, including:\n*   Date, time, and place of the assault.\n*   Whether she was drugged or intoxicated.\n*   Whether she washed, bathed, urinated, or changed clothes after the incident.\n\n#### 3. Physical Examination\n*   **General Physical:** Note her mental state, pulse, blood pressure, and signs of intoxication.\n*   **Clothing Inspection:** Check for tears, mud, bloodstains, and semen stains.\n*   **Systemic Injuries:** Carefully document general injuries (scratches, bruises, bite marks) on the neck, breasts, thighs, and buttocks, which indicate struggle.\n\n#### 4. Local Genital Examination\n*   Examine the vulva for swelling, bruising, or lacerations.\n*   Examine the hymen for fresh tears, bleeding, or old healed scars.\n*   Inspect the vaginal canal for tenderness, tears, or foreign matter. A speculum examination is avoided in virgins or if there is severe pain.\n\n---\n\n### c) Materials to be Collected for Laboratory Investigations\nTo ensure a robust chain of custody, the following samples must be collected, packaged, sealed, and sent to the Forensic Science Laboratory (FSL):\n1.  **Vaginal Swabs and Smears:** Two vaginal swabs and slides to detect spermatozoa and acid phosphatase.\n2.  **Pubic Hair:** Combings of pubic hair (to catch suspect's hairs) and control samples of the victim's pubic hair.\n3.  **Nail Scrapings:** From the victim's fingernails, to secure the suspect's skin cells/DNA from struggle.\n4.  **Blood and Urine Samples:** Collected in preservative tubes for toxicology (to rule out date-rape drugs) and DNA reference.\n5.  **Clothing:** All clothes worn during or immediately after the assault, dried and packed in separate paper bags.\n6.  **Swabs from other sites:** Oral, anal, or breast swabs if history indicates contact at these sites.",
