    "text": "Write In Detail About POCSO Act 2012.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Sexual Offences",
    "answer": "### Introduction\nThe **Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012**, is a comprehensive, progressive, and gender-neutral legislation enacted in India. Its primary objective is to protect children from sexual assault, sexual harassment, and pornography, while establishing child-friendly procedures for the reporting, investigation, and trial of these offenses.\n\n---\n\n### Key Features of the POCSO Act\n\n#### 1. Definition of a Child\nA child is legally defined as any person under **18 years of age**. The Act is completely gender-neutral, protecting both boys and girls equally.\n\n#### 2. Classification of Offenses\nThe Act defines and categorizes various offenses, prescribing stringent punishments:\n*   **Penetrative Sexual Assault:** Insertion of penis, object, or body part into vagina, urethra, anus, or mouth.\n*   **Aggravated Penetrative Sexual Assault:** Committed by persons in positions of trust or authority (e.g., police officers, teachers, doctors, guardians) or on disabled/pregnant children.\n*   **Sexual Assault & Harassment:** Non-penetrative sexual contact and sexual gestures.\n*   **Child Pornography:** Using children for pornographic purposes.\n\n#### 3. Mandatory Reporting (Section 19)\nAny person who has knowledge or apprehension that a sexual offense has been committed against a child is legally mandated to report it to the Special Juvenile Police Unit (SJPU) or the local police. Failure to report is a punishable offense.\n\n#### 4. Child-Friendly Procedures\n*   **No Uniforms:** Police officers recording the child's statement must not wear uniforms.\n*   **Safe Environment:** Statements must be recorded at the child’s residence or a place of their comfort.\n*   **No Contact:** The child must not come into contact with the accused at any stage.\n*   **Special Courts:** In-camera trials conducted by designated Special Courts to minimize secondary trauma.\n*   **Presumption of Guilt (Section 29):** Once the prosecution establishes the prima facie act of sexual abuse, the court presumes the accused is guilty, shifting the burden of proof to the defense.",
