    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Sexual Offences",
    "answer": "### Overview\nMasochism and Sadism are psychosexual disorders (paraphilias) where sexual arousal and gratification are linked to the experience or infliction of pain, suffering, or humiliation.\n\n### Sadism\n- Named after the Marquis de Sade.\n- A condition in which an individual derives sexual pleasure and excitement from **inflicting physical or psychological pain, suffering, or humiliation** on their partner.\n- **Forensic Significance**: It is frequently associated with domestic violence, sexual assault, and lust-murders (where victims are severely mutilated).\n\n### Masochism\n- Named after Leopold von Sacher-Masoch.\n- A condition in which sexual gratification is obtained by **experiencing pain, physical bondage, or humiliation** inflicted by oneself or by a partner.\n- **Forensic Significance**: Masochists may engage in dangerous practices like autoerotic asphyxiation (hanging or chest compression to cause hypoxia), which can lead to accidental death. These cases are often mistaken for suicide or homicide.",
