    "text": "Add a note on MACERATION  & it's Signs.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Infanticide & Child Abuse",
    "answer": "### Definition\nMaceration is an aseptic autolytic decomposition of a dead fetus in the uterus, occurring in the presence of sterile amniotic fluid without the entry of air or bacteria. It occurs when a fetus dies in utero and remains inside for at least 24 hours before delivery.\n\n### Chronological Signs of Maceration\n- **At 24 hours**: Large, flaccid blisters (blebs) containing reddish, sterile fluid appear on the skin. Skin begins to slip (desquamation) leaving raw, red, moist, coppery-red areas.\n- **At 36 to 48 hours**: Skin slippage becomes extensive. The body becomes extremely soft, flaccid, and loses its shape (distorted posture).\n- **At 3 to 4 days**: Complete softening of ligaments and splitting of joints occurs. The cranial bones overlap due to liquefaction of the brain, a radiological feature known as **Spalding's sign**.\n- **At 7 to 8 days**: Internal organs are soft and liquefying. The pleural, pericardial, and peritoneal cavities contain sterile, reddish-brown fluid. The brain becomes a semi-fluid greyish-red mass.\n\n### Characteristic Features\n- **Color**: The skin is dull, reddish-brown, or coppery.\n- **Odor**: A characteristic sweetish, disagreeable odor (unlike the foul, putrid odor of bacterial decomposition).\n- **Sterility**: The tissues and amniotic fluid remain sterile.\n\n### Medico-legal Importance\n1. **Absolute Proof of Stillbirth**: It is conclusive evidence of intrauterine death, excluding any possibility of live birth.\n2. **Estimation of Time since Death**: The extent of maceration helps estimate the duration of time the dead fetus remained in the uterus.",
