    "text": "Signs of Dead Born Foetus.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Infanticide & Child Abuse",
    "answer": "### Introduction\nA dead-born (stillborn) fetus is one that dies in the uterus before birth (intrauterine death) or dies during the process of birth before being completely born, exhibiting no signs of life (breathing, heartbeat, cord pulsation, or voluntary movements) after complete expulsion or extraction.\n\n### Signs of Intrauterine Death before Labor\nIf the fetus dies in utero days before delivery, it exhibits characteristic decomposition changes:\n1. **Maceration**: Aseptic autolytic decomposition in amniotic fluid.\n   - Desquamation (skin slippage) within 24 hours.\n   - Soft, flaccid, coppery-red body with a sweetish, disagreeable odor.\n   - **Spalding's Sign**: Overlapping of cranial bones on radiological examination due to brain liquefaction.\n2. **Mummification**: Occurs if amniotic fluid is deficient or rapidly absorbed. The fetus is shriveled, dry, greyish-yellow, and leathery, without any odor.\n\n### Signs of Death during Labor (Stillbirth)\nIf death occurs during labor, the fetus is fresh but lacks signs of respiration and life:\n1. **Lack of Respiration (Pulmonary Signs)**:\n   - *Chest*: Flat, collapsed chest cavity with oblique ribs.\n   - *Lungs*: Small, unexpanded, dark red, firm, liver-like (hepatized), and sink in water (Hydrostatic test is negative).\n2. **Circulatory Signs**: No drying, shriveling, or line of demarcation around the insertion site of the umbilical cord.\n3. **Gastrointestinal Signs**: No air in the stomach or intestines (Breslau's second life test is negative). The organs sink in water.\n4. **Middle Ear**: Contains embryonic gelatinous tissue instead of air (Wrede's test is negative).",
