    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Infanticide & Child Abuse",
    "answer": "### Definition and Principle\nThe Hydrostatic Test (Raygat's test) is a classic postmortem test performed on a newborn to determine whether the child was born alive (respired) or dead (stillborn/unrespired). It is based on the physical principle that lungs that have breathed contain air, lowering their specific gravity (about 0.94) and causing them to float in water. Unrespired lungs are airless, have a higher specific gravity (about 1.05), and sink.\n\n### Procedure\n1. **Ligation**: The trachea is tied securely to prevent any escape of air or entry of water.\n2. **First Stage**: The entire thoracic block (heart, lungs, and thymus) is removed together and placed in a container of clean water. If it floats, it suggests respiration.\n3. **Second Stage**: The lungs are separated from the heart and tested individually in water.\n4. **Third Stage**: Each lung is cut into 12 to 20 small pieces. Each piece is tested for flotation.\n5. **Fourth Stage (Compression Test)**: The pieces are subjected to firm pressure (squeezed between the folds of a cloth or under a wooden block) to expel any gas of putrefaction, and re-tested. Respired pieces will continue to float because alveolar air cannot be fully expelled, whereas putrefied pieces will sink.\n\n### False Positives (Floats but Dead Born)\n- **Decomposition**: Putrefactive gas causes flotation. *Correction*: Squeezing the lung pieces expels putrefactive gas, causing them to sink.\n- **Artificial Respiration**: Air is introduced mechanically.\n\n### False Negatives (Sinks but Live Born)\n- **Atelectasis**: Congenital failure of lungs to expand.\n- **Pathology**: Extreme pneumonia, pulmonary edema, or hyaline membrane disease.\n- **Suffocation**: Murdered before taking the first breath.",
