    "id": 193,
    "text": "Write Short note On BATTERED BABY SYNDROME.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Infanticide & Child Abuse",
    "answer": "### Definition\nBattered Baby Syndrome (also known as Caffey's syndrome or Non-Accidental Injury in Children) is a clinical condition in young children (usually under 3 years of age) who have received serious physical abuse, resulting in multiple, non-accidental injuries inflicted by a parent or guardian.\n\n### Clinical Features & Signs\n1. **History Discrepancy**: The explanation given by parents for the injuries is inconsistent with the severity, type, or age of the injuries observed.\n2. **Multiple Injuries at Different Stages of Healing**:\n   - *Bruises/Contusions*: Various colors (blue, yellow, green) indicating injuries occurred at different times.\n   - *Burns*: Cigarette burns, scalds (doughnut-shaped burns on buttocks/perineum from immersion in hot water).\n   - *Bite marks*: Documented using photography and swabs for DNA.\n3. **Radiological Findings (Pathognomonic)**:\n   - Multiple fractures in different stages of healing.\n   - **Metaphyseal bucket-handle fractures** (due to twisting/pulling of limbs).\n   - Subperiosteal new bone formation.\n   - Multiple rib fractures.\n4. **Visceral Injuries**: Rupture of the liver, spleen, or bowel, and subdural hematomas.\n\n### Medico-legal Importance\n- **Child Protection**: The doctor's primary duty is to protect the child by immediate hospitalization and notification to child protection agencies.\n- **Legal Action**: Documenting injuries serves as crucial evidence for prosecution under the **Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act** and relevant sections of the IPC/BNS.",
