    "id": 196,
    "text": "Breslau's Second life test.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Infanticide & Child Abuse",
    "answer": "### Definition and Principle\nBreslau's Second Life Test (also known as the stomach-bowel test) is a corroborative postmortem test used to differentiate between a live-born and dead-born child. It is based on the principle that a child born alive swallows air during its first breaths, which enters the stomach and subsequently passes into the intestines.\n\n### Procedure\n- Double ligatures are placed at the cardiac end of the stomach and the lower end of the rectum to prevent gas escape.\n- The stomach and intestines are removed together and placed in water.\n- If the entire gastrointestinal tract floats, it indicates a live birth.\n- The organs can be separated and tested individually to trace how far down the air has traveled (e.g., stomach only, duodenum, or small intestines), which helps estimate the duration of survival.\n\n### Limitations\nFalse positives can occur due to putrefactive gas formation or artificial respiration.",
