    "text": "Shaking baby syndrome./Infantile Whiplash syndrome.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Infanticide & Child Abuse",
    "answer": "### Definition\nShaken Baby Syndrome (SBS), also known as Infantile Whiplash-Shake Syndrome or Abusive Head Trauma (AHT), is a severe form of physical child abuse caused by violently shaking an infant or young child (usually under 2 years).\n\n### Mechanism\nThe infant's neck muscles are weak and the head is relatively large and heavy. Violent shaking causes rapid acceleration-deceleration of the brain, leading to rotational forces that tear bridging veins and damage brain tissue, without necessarily showing external signs of head injury.\n\n### Classic Triad\n1. **Subdural hemorrhage (SDH)**\n2. **Retinal hemorrhages** (pathognomonic, often bilateral and extensive)\n3. **Encephalopathy** (cerebral edema, hypoxia)\n\n### Forensic Significance\nIt is a major cause of morbidity and mortality in infants. Autopsy reveals the triad and sometimes occult posterior rib fractures.",
