    "text": "Sudden Infant death syndrome.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Infanticide & Child Abuse",
    "answer": "### Definition\nSudden Infant Death Syndrome (SIDS), also known as \"crib death\" or \"cot death\", is the sudden and unexplained death of an infant under 1 year of age, which remains unexplained even after a thorough case investigation, including a complete autopsy, examination of the death scene, and review of the clinical history.\n\n### Risk Factors\nProne sleeping position, soft bedding, maternal smoking during pregnancy, overheating, and premature birth.\n\n### Autopsy Findings (typically non-specific/negative)\n- Petechial hemorrhages on the intrathoracic organs (thymus, pleura, epicardium).\n- Congestion and mild edema of the lungs.\n- Empty bladder.\n\n### Forensic Importance\nSIDS is a diagnosis of exclusion. A meticulous autopsy is essential to rule out mechanical suffocation, infanticide, child abuse, and silent infections.",
