    "text": "What is Caput succedaneum.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Infanticide & Child Abuse",
    "answer": "### Definition\nCaput succedaneum is a localized, diffuse, serosanguinous fluid swelling of the scalp tissues, caused by pressure on the presenting part of the fetal head against the cervix during labor.\n\n### Features\n- It is present at the time of birth.\n- It crosses the cranial suture lines (unlike cephalhematoma).\n- It is soft, boggy, pits on pressure, and resolves spontaneously within 24 to 48 hours without treatment.\n\n### Forensic Significance\n- It indicates that the child was alive during labor (vital reaction to birth canal pressure).\n- Its presence confirms head presentation.\n- It must be differentiated from cephalhematoma, which is a subperiosteal hemorrhage that does *not* cross suture lines and appears hours after birth.",
