    "id": 201,
    "text": "Add a note on LOCARD'S PRINCIPLES OF EXCHANGE.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Trace Evidence & Forensic Science",
    "answer": "### Definition and Principle\nLocard's Principle of Exchange is the cornerstone of modern forensic science, formulated by the French pioneer Edmond Locard. It states: **\"Every contact leaves a trace.\"** \n\n### Mechanism\nWhen two objects or individuals come into contact with each other, there is a mutual and reciprocal transfer of physical or biological material between them.\n\n### Application in Crime Scenes\n- A criminal will always bring something to the crime scene and leave with something from it (e.g., fibers from clothes, hair, fingerprints, DNA, dust, mud, or glass fragments).\n- Forensic investigators search for these trace evidences to link a suspect to the victim, the crime scene, or a weapon.\n\n### Forensic Significance\nIt forms the scientific basis for trace evidence analysis and emphasizes the importance of preserving the crime scene to prevent contamination.",
