    "id": 202,
    "text": "Tests For Blood Stains.(Luminol,Teichman,Takayama Test -3marks)",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Trace Evidence & Forensic Science",
    "answer": "### 1. Luminol Test (Presumptive/Screening)\n- **Principle**: A highly sensitive chemical test used to detect latent or washed bloodstains. The luminol reagent reacts with iron in hemoglobin, producing a bright blue chemiluminescence visible in the dark.\n- **Note**: It is non-destructive but can give false positives with bleach or plant peroxidases.\n\n### 2. Teichman Test (Confirmatory/Microcrystalline)\n- **Principle**: The blood stain is treated with glacial acetic acid and a halide salt (NaCl) and heated. Under a microscope, dark brown, rhomboid crystals of **hemin** (halogenated hematin) confirm the presence of blood.\n\n### 3. Takayama Test (Confirmatory/Microcrystalline)\n- **Principle**: The stain is heated with a reagent containing pyridine, glucose, and sodium hydroxide. The formation of pink, feathery, or needle-like crystals of **pyridine hemochromogen** confirms blood.",
