    "id": 203,
    "text": "Chemical Examination of Seminal Fluids.(Florence, Barberios , Acid phospatase test)",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Trace Evidence & Forensic Science",
    "answer": "### 1. Florence Test (Presumptive)\n- **Principle**: The stain extract is treated with Florence's reagent (iodine in potassium iodide). The formation of dark brown, rhombic, needle-like or star-shaped crystals of **choline periodide** indicates semen. It detects choline.\n\n### 2. Barberio's Test (Presumptive)\n- **Principle**: The extract is treated with saturated picric acid. The formation of yellow, needle-like or ovoid crystals of **spermine picrate** indicates semen. It detects spermine.\n\n### 3. Acid Phosphatase (AP) Test (Confirmatory/Enzymatic)\n- **Principle**: Semen contains very high levels of acid phosphatase secreted by the prostate gland. The reagent (alpha-naphthyl phosphate and Brentamine Fast Blue) produces a rapid, deep purple color within 30 seconds.\n- **Note**: It is highly specific and works even in aspermic or vasectomized males.",
