    "text": "POLYGRAPH (3 m)",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Trace Evidence & Forensic Science",
    "answer": "### Definition\nA Polygraph, commonly known as a lie detector, is an instrument that measures and records physiological changes in a subject's body while they are asked a series of questions.\n\n### Physiological Parameters Monitored\n1. **Respiration rate and depth** (using pneumographs around chest/abdomen)\n2. **Cardiovascular activity** (blood pressure and heart rate using a cuff)\n3. **Galvanic Skin Response (GSR)** / Electrodermal activity (sweating measured via electrodes on fingers).\n\n### Principle\nLying is accompanied by emotional stress, which triggers a sympathetic nervous system response (fight-or-flight), causing involuntary physiological changes that are recorded as spikes on a chart.\n\n### Legal Aspect\nLike narco-analysis, involuntary polygraph testing is unconstitutional (Selvi case, 2010). The test requires voluntary consent and the results are not admissible as substantive evidence in a court of law, but can be used as investigative aids.",
