    "id": 212,
    "text": "UNIVERSAL ANTIDOTE",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "General Toxicology",
    "answer": "### Description\nThe Universal Antidote is a physical/mechanical and chemical antidote mixture used in emergency toxicology when the exact nature of the ingested poison is unknown or when multiple poisons have been ingested.\n\n### Composition\n1. **Activated Charcoal (2 parts)**: Acts as a physical adsorbent, binding alkaloids, glycosides, and many organic poisons.\n2. **Magnesium Oxide (1 part)**: Acts as a chemical antidote, neutralizing acids and precipitating heavy metals.\n3. **Tannic Acid (1 part)**: Acts as a chemical antidote, precipitating alkaloids, glucosides, and some heavy metals into insoluble tannates.\n\n### Administration\n1 tablespoon (approx 15-20g) of the mixture is stirred in a glass of warm water and administered orally or via gastric tube.\n\n### Modern Status\nAlthough historically popular, it has largely been replaced in modern toxicology by pure **Activated Charcoal**, which is highly superior in adsorbing power, as magnesium oxide and tannic acid can interfere with the adsorptive capacity of the charcoal.",
