    "text": "NPDS Act 1985",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "General Toxicology",
    "answer": "### Definition and Purpose\nThe Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances (NDPS) Act, 1985 (misspelled as NPDS in the question) is a comprehensive legislation in India enacted to control and regulate operations relating to narcotic drugs and psychotropic substances.\n\n### Key Provisions\n- Prohibits the cultivation, production, possession, sale, purchase, transport, warehousing, consumption, use, import, and export of narcotic drugs (e.g., opium, cannabis, heroin) and psychotropic substances (e.g., barbiturates, amphetamines, LSD) except for medical or scientific purposes.\n- Imposes strict, deterrent punishments, graded according to the quantity of the drug involved (small quantity, intermediate quantity, and commercial quantity).\n- Commercial quantities carry rigorous imprisonment of 10 to 20 years and heavy fines. Under Section 31A, subsequent convictions for commercial quantities could attract the death penalty under specific circumstances.\n- Provisions for search, seizure, arrest without warrant, and establishment of the Narcotics Control Bureau (NCB).",
