    "text": "Demulcents.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "General Toxicology",
    "answer": "### Definition and Mechanism\nDemulcents are inert substances administered orally that form a protective, soothing coating over the mucous membranes of the gastrointestinal tract, thereby reducing irritation, pain, and inflammation.\n- **Mechanism**: They line the stomach and esophageal walls, preventing direct contact and further absorption of remaining corrosive or irritant poisons.\n\n### Common Examples\nMilk, egg white (albumin), starch mucilage, barley water, olive oil, and liquid paraffin.\n\n### Indications\nIngestion of corrosive acids, alkalies, metallic irritants, or other highly irritating substances.\n\n### Contraindications\nDemulcents like fats and oils (e.g., castor oil, olive oil) are strictly contraindicated in cases of fat-soluble poisons like organophosphates, phosphorus, camphor, or kerosene, as they accelerate the absorption of these poisons in the gut.",
