    "text": "Name 3 chelating agents and the poisons to which they are Given.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "General Toxicology",
    "answer": "### Definition\nChelating agents are chemical antidotes that bind to heavy metals in the body to form stable, non-toxic, water-soluble ring complexes (chelates) that are easily excreted by the kidneys.\n\n### Three Key Chelating Agents & Indications\n1. **Dimercaprol (British Anti-Lewisite or BAL)**:\n   - *Given for*: Acute poisoning by **Arsenic**, **Mercury**, and **Gold** (sometimes as an adjunct in Lead poisoning). It is administered via deep intramuscular injection.\n2. **Penicillamine (D-Penicillamine)**:\n   - *Given for*: **Copper** poisoning (Wilson's disease), and chronic poisoning by **Lead** and **Mercury**. It is an oral chelating agent.\n3. **Edetate Calcium Disodium ($CaNa_2EDTA$)**:\n   - *Given for*: Acute and chronic **Lead** poisoning. It is administered intravenously (never use $Na_2EDTA$ alone as it causes severe hypocalcemia).",
