    "id": 218,
    "text": "Classify agricultural poisons. Describe in detail the signs, symptoms, mechanism of action, treatment, and postmortem findings in a case of Organophosphorus (OPC) poisoning.",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "Pesticides",
    "answer": "### Definition\nAgricultural poisons (pesticides) are chemical substances used to control, destroy, or repel pests. They are classified based on the target pests they destroy:\n\n### Classification and Examples\n1. **Insecticides** (control insects):\n   - *Organophosphorus compounds (OPCs)*: Malathion, Parathion, Chlorpyrifos, Diazinon.\n   - *Organochlorines*: DDT, Endosulfan, Lindane, Endrin (\"Plant Penicillin\").\n   - *Carbamates*: Carbaryl, Propoxur, Carbofuran.\n   - *Synthetic Pyrethroids*: Allethrin, Cypermethrin, Deltamethrin.\n2. **Herbicides** (kill weeds): Paraquat, Diquat, 2,4-D.\n3. **Rodenticides** (kill rodents): Zinc phosphide, Barium carbonate, Warfarin, Strychnine.\n4. **Fungicides** (kill fungi): Organomercurials, Thiram, Bordeaux mixture.\n5. **Fumigants** (gaseous agents): Aluminum phosphide (Quickphos), Ethylene dibromide.\n\n---\n\n### Signs & Symptoms\nArise from acetylcholinesterase inhibition, causing acetylcholine accumulation (cholinergic crisis). \n- **Muscarinic**: Pinpoint pupils (miosis), excessive salivation, lacrimation, sweating, bradycardia, bronchospasm, abdominal colic, diarrhea.\n- **Nicotinic**: Muscle twitching, fasciculations, weakness, hypertension.\n- **CNS**: Headache, confusion, convulsions, coma.\n\n### Treatment\n1. **Airway** management, oxygenation, and skin decontamination.\n2. **Atropine** (Antimuscarinic): Dose 2-5 mg IV every 5-10 minutes until \"atropinization\" (dry mouth, dilated pupils, heart rate >100 bpm).\n3. **Pralidoxime (PAM / Oximes)** (Cholinesterase reactivator): 1-2 g IV slow infusion; must be given before \"aging\" of the enzyme.\n\n### Postmortem Findings\n- Garlic-like odor from tissues and stomach.\n- Severe congestion of all internal organs.\n- Froth at the mouth and nose.\n- Pinpoint pupils.\n- Pulmonary edema and subpleural hemorrhages.",
    "stars": 5
