    "id": 219,
    "text": "Plant Penicillin Poisoning (Endrin)",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Pesticides",
    "answer": "### Description\n**Endrin** is an organochlorine insecticide of the chlorinated cyclodiene group. In rural India, it is colloquially termed **\"Plant Penicillin\"** by farmers because of its highly effective, broad-spectrum insecticidal properties, leading them to use it as a cure-all remedy for crops.\n\n### Toxicity and Mechanism\nIt is a highly toxic, neurotoxic compound. It acts by blocking GABA-gated chloride channels in the central nervous system, leading to hyper-excitability and severe convulsions.\n\n### Clinical Features\nRapid onset of action. Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, dizziness, hyper-reflexia, and violent **epileptiform convulsions** (grand mal seizures) followed by coma and respiratory failure.\n\n### Treatment\nThere is no specific antidote. Treatment is supportive, focusing on controlling seizures using **Benzodiazepines** (Diazepam/Midazolam) or barbiturates, maintaining the airway, and gastric lavage (if within 1 hour).\n\n### Forensic Significance\nFrequently used for suicide in rural areas, and occasionally accidental due to crop spraying without protection.",
