    "id": 223,
    "text": "Differences Between Arsenic Poisoning And Cholera.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Metallic & Non-Metallic Irritants",
    "answer": "### Introduction\nAcute arsenic poisoning is notoriously known to mimic Cholera (an infectious disease caused by *Vibrio cholerae*) because both present with severe vomiting, watery diarrhea, dehydration, and cardiovascular collapse. Differentiating them is vital in both clinical medicine and forensic investigations.\n\n### Comparison Table\n| Feature | Acute Arsenic Poisoning | Cholera |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **History/Circumstance** | Sudden onset after ingestion of food/drink. Often suspected homicide/suicide. | Occurs during epidemics or in endemic areas with poor sanitation. |\n| **Onset of Symptoms** | Rapid (usually within 30-60 mins). | Gradual (incubation period of 1-3 days). |\n| **Sequence of Symptoms** | **Vomiting precedes purging (diarrhea)**. | **Purging (diarrhea) precedes vomiting**. |\n| **Throat Symptoms** | Intense burning pain in the throat, dysphagia (difficulty swallowing). | Throat burning and dysphagia are absent. |\n| **Stools** | Watery, contain blood-stained mucus, and have a metallic or garlic taste/odor. | **\"Rice-water\" stools**, watery, colorless, with a fishy odor, rarely containing blood. |\n| **Pain** | Severe abdominal colic and muscle cramps. | Abdominal pain is usually absent or very mild; muscle cramps due to dehydration. |\n| **Tenesmus** | Severe (painful urge to defecate). | Absent. |\n| **Feces Toxicology/Microbiology** | Toxicological analysis of gastric wash/stools reveals **Arsenic**. | Stool microscopy/culture reveals **Vibrio cholerae**. |\n| **Autopsy Findings** | Gastric mucosa shows **\"red velvet\"** appearance, subendocardial hemorrhages in left ventricle. | No mucosal inflammation; intestines contain cholera fluid, standard dehydration signs. |",
