    "id": 226,
    "text": "Phossy Jaw",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Metallic & Non-Metallic Irritants",
    "answer": "### Definition\n**Phossy Jaw** (phosphorus necrosis of the jaw) is a chronic, painful occupational disease caused by long-term inhalation or ingestion of **Yellow (White) Phosphorus** fumes, historically prevalent in match industry workers.\n\n### Pathophysiology\nPhosphorus enters through diseased gums or decayed teeth, causing painful periostitis, osteitis, and thrombosis of local blood vessels (ischemia). Secondary bacterial infection leads to osteonecrosis of the mandible (lower jaw).\n\n### Clinical Features\nToothache, swollen gums, abscesses, and discharging sinuses. The necrotic bone separates as a lightweight, porous sequestrum resembling **pumice stone**, which may exhibit **phosphorescence** (glowing in the dark).\n\n### Prevention & Modern Status\nBanned globally by the 1906 Berne Convention, replacing yellow phosphorus with safe red phosphorus.\n\n### Treatment\nSurgical resection of the necrotic bone (sequestrectomy) and aggressive antibiotic therapy.",
