    "id": 227,
    "text": "Rain Drop Pigmentation.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Metallic & Non-Metallic Irritants",
    "answer": "### Definition & Cause\n**Rain drop pigmentation** is a classic skin manifestation associated with chronic arsenic poisoning (arsenicosis). It arises due to the stimulatory effect of arsenic on melanin synthesis within the epidermal basal cells.\n\n### Appearance\nIt is characterized by multiple, small (usually 2 to 10 mm in diameter), circular, dark brown or black hyperpigmented macules. These dark spots are interspersed with areas of normal or relatively hypopigmented skin, creating a distinct aesthetic that closely resembles **\"rain-drops on a dusty road\"**.\n\n### Distribution\nThis pigmentation is most prominent on the chest, back, abdomen, and flanks (the trunk). It can also involve the extremities (arms and legs) but typically spares the face, palms, and soles.\n\n### Diagnostic Significance\nIt serves as a critical diagnostic hallmark of chronic arsenic toxicity, appearing months to years after prolonged exposure (e.g., via contaminated drinking water). It is often accompanied by arsenical keratosis (rough, hyperkeratotic plaques on the palms and soles) and is considered a premalignant lesion that elevates the risk of developing skin malignancies, such as Bowen's disease or squamous cell carcinoma.",
