    "text": "Arsenicosis & Arsenophagists.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Metallic & Non-Metallic Irritants",
    "answer": "### Arsenicosis\n**Arsenicosis** is the clinical syndrome of chronic arsenic poisoning resulting from prolonged exposure (usually over months or years) to arsenic. The most common source is the ingestion of contaminated tube-well drinking water containing arsenic levels exceeding the WHO limit (>0.01 mg/L, or the Indian standard of >0.05 mg/L). \n\n**Clinical Features:** It is characterized by a classic triad of hyperpigmentation (rain-drop pigmentation), palmar and plantar hyperkeratosis (arsenical keratosis), and hepatomegaly, which can progress to non-cirrhotic portal hypertension. Peripheral vascular involvement can lead to gangrene of the feet, known as **Blackfoot disease**. Systemic effects include peripheral neuropathy and a high incidence of internal malignancies (lungs, bladder, kidney, liver).\n\n### Arsenophagists\n**Arsenophagists** (literally \"arsenic eaters\") are individuals who habitually ingest small, increasing doses of arsenic (usually arsenic trioxide) over a long period. Historically documented in the Styria region of Austria, these individuals develop an acquired tolerance to the toxic effects of the metal. \n\n**Purpose & MLI:** They consume arsenic to improve their complexion, increase respiratory stamina at high altitudes, and boost physical strength. They can tolerate doses up to 300 to 400 mg (well above the typical fatal dose of 120 to 200 mg) without showing signs of acute poisoning. However, abrupt withdrawal can induce severe systemic illness or latent toxicity, and their viscera at autopsy may contain high concentrations of arsenic despite no immediate cause of death from acute toxicity.",
