    "id": 229,
    "text": "Mee's Line /Aldrich Line /Reynolds Line.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Metallic & Non-Metallic Irritants",
    "answer": "### Definition & Nomenclature\n**Mee's lines** (also referred to as **Aldrich-Mee's lines** or **Reynolds' lines**) are transverse, single or multiple, white bands that run across the fingernails and toenails, oriented parallel to the lunula.\n\n### Etiology\nWhile classically diagnostic of **acute or chronic arsenic poisoning**, they are not entirely pathognomonic. They can also occur in heavy metal poisoning (such as thallium, selenium, and lead), carbon monoxide exposure, renal failure, chemotherapy, malaria, and severe systemic febrile illnesses.\n\n### Pathophysiology & Forensic Significance\nThese lines are caused by a temporary disruption of keratin synthesis and nail plate growth in the nail matrix due to the toxic insult. Because nails grow at a relatively constant rate of approximately **0.1 mm per day** (or about 3 mm per month), the distance of the white line from the proximal nail fold (cuticle) can be measured to estimate the approximate date or timeframe when the toxic exposure occurred.\n\n### Characteristics\nUnlike leukonychia due to trauma, Mee's lines affect multiple nails simultaneously, do not disappear when digital pressure is applied, and gradually migrate distally as the nail grows.",
