    "text": "Add A Note On XANTHOPROTEIC REACTION & Bed Site Test",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Corrosives",
    "answer": "### Xanthoproteic Reaction\nThe **xanthoproteic reaction** is a specific chemical reaction and a vital diagnostic finding characteristic of **nitric acid ($HNO_3$)** exposure on tissues. Nitric acid reacts with proteins containing aromatic amino acids (such as tyrosine and tryptophan) present in the skin, nails, and mucous membranes. \n\nThis reaction leads to the nitration of these amino acid residues, forming a bright yellow compound known as **xanthoproteic acid**. If an alkaline substance (such as ammonium hydroxide or sodium hydroxide) is added to this yellow stain, the color shifts to a deep orange. This reaction explains why nitric acid spills produce a permanent, non-washable yellow discoloration of the skin and mucosal surfaces.\n\n### Bedside Test for Corrosives\nIn cases of suspected corrosive poisoning, a quick bedside test can be performed on the vomitus or gastric aspirate:\n1. **Litmus Paper Test:** A drop of the vomitus is placed on litmus paper. A shift from blue to red confirms a strong acid, whereas red to blue indicates a strong alkali.\n2. **Copper Turnings Test (for Nitric Acid):** A small quantity of the gastric contents is heated with copper turnings in a test tube. The evolution of dense, irritating brown fumes of nitrogen dioxide ($NO_2$) confirms the presence of nitric acid.",
