    "id": 232,
    "text": "Short Note On CARBOLURIA( Phenol Poisoning).",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Corrosives",
    "answer": "### Definition and Clinical Concept\n**Carboluria** is a classical diagnostic sign of phenol (carbolic acid) poisoning, characterized by the excretion of dark-greenish, smoky-brown, or blackish colored urine.\n\n### Biochemical Mechanism\nWhen phenol is absorbed into the body (via ingestion, inhalation, or skin absorption), it undergoes hepatic metabolism. In the liver, it is conjugated with sulfate and glucuronic acid. A portion of the absorbed phenol is oxidized into two major metabolites: **hydroquinone (quinol)** and **pyrocatechol (catechol)**. \n\nWhen urine is freshly voided by the patient, it may appear normal in color or only slightly dark. However, upon standing in the air and being exposed to atmospheric oxygen, these excreted metabolites (hydroquinone and pyrocatechol) undergo spontaneous oxidation. The oxidation products impart a characteristic dark-greenish to smoky-black hue to the urine.\n\n### Clinical & Medico-Legal Significance\nCarboluria is a highly pathognomonic sign of systemic phenol poisoning (carbolism). It is usually accompanied by other features such as a characteristic phenolic odor of the breath, oliguria, albuminuria, intravascular hemolysis, methemoglobinemia, and acute renal failure due to tubular necrosis. Its presence demands immediate supportive therapy and renal function monitoring.",
