    "id": 233,
    "text": "Oxalic Acid Poisoning & Treatment. (3 M)",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Corrosives",
    "answer": "### Toxicology & Mechanism\n**Oxalic acid** is a corrosive organic acid found in household cleaning products (such as rust and ink stain removers). Locally, it acts as a mild corrosive. Systemically, it is rapidly absorbed and binds to ionized calcium in the blood, leading to **severe hypocalcemia**. It also forms insoluble calcium oxalate crystals which precipitate in the renal tubules, causing acute tubular necrosis.\n\n### Signs & Symptoms\nIngestion causes burning pain in the mouth and abdomen, dark green or bloody vomiting, and features of acute hypocalcemia (tetany, muscle spasms, carpopedal spasm, hyperreflexia, and cardiac arrhythmias). Renal involvement leads to oliguria, hematuria, and uremic coma.\n\n### Treatment Protocols\n1. **Strict Contraindication:** **Do NOT use sodium bicarbonate** or other soluble alkalis, as they form soluble oxalates which are more easily absorbed, worsening systemic toxicity.\n2. **Specific Local Antidote:** Administer **Calcium Lactate, Calcium Gluconate, or Lime Water (Calcium hydroxide)** orally or via gastric lavage. This precipitates the oxalic acid in the stomach into insoluble calcium oxalate, preventing systemic absorption.\n3. **Systemic Management:** Administer **10% Calcium Gluconate** (10 ml IV slowly) to correct life-threatening hypocalcemia. Repeat as guided by serum calcium levels.\n4. **Renal Protection:** Maintain high urine output via IV fluids and diuretics to prevent renal tubular deposition of crystals. Hemodialysis is indicated in cases of acute renal failure.",
