    "id": 234,
    "text": "Short Note On Phytotoxin/ Toxalbumin .",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Plant & Animal Irritants",
    "answer": "### Definition\n**Phytotoxins** (specifically **toxalbumins**) are highly toxic, plant-derived proteins that share structural, functional, and antigenic properties with certain bacterial exotoxins (such as diphtheria toxin) and animal venoms.\n\n### High-Yield Examples\n1. **Ricin:** Extracted from the seeds of *Ricinus communis* (Castor oil plant).\n2. **Abrin:** Extracted from the seeds of *Abrus precatorius* (Jequirity bean / Rati).\n3. **Crotin:** Extracted from *Croton tiglium* seeds.\n\n### Mechanism of Action\nToxalbumins are classified as **Ribosome Inactivating Proteins (RIPs)**. They consist of two polypeptide chains (A and B) linked by a disulfide bond. Chain B binds to carbohydrates on the cell surface, facilitating the entry of Chain A into the cytoplasm. Chain A then enzymatically cleaves ribosomal RNA (inactivating the 60S ribosomal subunit), which halts cellular protein synthesis, resulting in rapid cell death.\n\n### Clinical Effects & Antigenicity\nIngestion or injection causes severe hemorrhagic gastroenteritis, local tissue necrosis, intravascular hemolysis, and multi-organ failure. They are highly antigenic; sublethal doses can stimulate the production of specific antibodies (antitoxins) in animals, a feature unique to these plant proteins.",
