    "id": 235,
    "text": "Pseudo Bruise & It's MLI",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Plant & Animal Irritants",
    "answer": "### Definition\nA **pseudo bruise** (or false bruise) is a localized skin lesion or inflammatory reaction that clinically resembles a mechanical contusion (bruise), but is actually caused by the deliberate application of chemical irritants or plant juices to the skin.\n\n### Etiology\nIt is most commonly produced by applying the juice or paste of the **marking nut (*Semecarpus anacardium* / Bhilawan)** or the milky sap of **Calotropis** directly onto the skin.\n\n### Differentiating Features\n* **Pseudo Bruise:** Characterized by the presence of small blisters or vesicles filled with acrid, irritating fluid; margins are highly irregular and sharply defined; it is accompanied by intense itching and burning; and it **does not** undergo the sequential color changes of hemoglobin breakdown (red -> blue -> green -> yellow). Chemical analysis of the skin swab can detect anacardic acid or calotropin.\n* **True Bruise:** Shows subcutaneous extravasation of blood, diffuse margins, no vesicles or acrid fluid, and undergoes classic chronological color changes.\n\n### Medico-Legal Importance (MLI)\n1. **False Allegations:** Fabricated by individuals to bring false charges of assault or physical torture against their enemies.\n2. **Malingering:** Used by prisoners to escape hard labor, avoid military service, or gain admission into hospitals.",
