    "id": 236,
    "text": "ABRUS PRECATORIOUS/Rati Sui",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Plant & Animal Irritants",
    "answer": "### Identity & Active Principle\n***Abrus precatorius*** (commonly known as Rati, Gunchi, or Jequirity bean) is a climbing shrub. Its seeds are small, hard, and highly distinctive, featuring a bright scarlet-red body with a black spot at one end. The active toxic principle is **abrin**, an extremely potent toxalbumin/phytotoxin.\n\n### Rati Sui (Poisonous Spikes)\nIn rural India, the seeds are used criminally to prepare **\"sui\" (needles or spikes)**. The decorticated seed kernels are powdered, mixed with water and opium or onion paste, and rolled into sharp, needle-like spikes (5 to 15 mm long). These spikes are dried in the sun until hard and are then fitted onto a wooden handle.\n\n### Mechanism & Clinical Use\nThese sui are struck into the skin of cattle or sleeping humans. The moisture of the subcutaneous tissues dissolves the abrin, which enters the circulation. It causes severe local inflammation, painful swelling, necrosis, regional lymphadenopathy, and systemic intravascular hemolysis. The clinical picture mimics a **viperine snake bite**. Death occurs within 24 to 48 hours due to cardiac failure and shock.",
