    "id": 243,
    "text": "Describe The Clinical Features Treatment And Postmortem Appearances Of METHANOL POISONING",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "CNS Depressants",
    "answer": "### Introduction & Metabolism\n**Methanol** (methyl alcohol, wood alcohol, or wood spirit) is a highly toxic, colorless industrial solvent. It is a common and lethal adulterant in illicitly distilled liquor (resulting in \"hooch tragedies\").\n\n#### Toxicokinetics\nMethanol itself is relatively non-toxic and causes mild CNS depression similar to ethanol. However, it is slowly metabolized in the liver by the enzyme **alcohol dehydrogenase** to **formaldehyde**, which is then rapidly metabolized by **aldehyde dehydrogenase** to **formic acid**. Formic acid is the primary toxic metabolite. It inhibits mitochondrial cytochrome oxidase, causing cellular hypoxia, profound metabolic acidosis, and selective damage to the optic nerve and basal ganglia (putamen).\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Features\n1. **Latent Period:** A characteristic symptom-free lag phase of **12 to 24 hours** (up to 30 hours) occurs while toxic formic acid accumulates.\n2. **Gastrointestinal:** Severe abdominal pain (due to acute pancreatitis), nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea.\n3. **Central Nervous System:** Headache, vertigo, dizziness, confusion, seizures, Parkinsonian-like tremors (due to bilateral putaminal necrosis), and coma.\n4. **Ocular Toxicity (\"Snowstorm Vision\"):** Formic acid selectively damages the retinal ganglion cells and optic nerve. Symptoms include photophobia, blurred vision, and the classic sensation of \"standing in a snowstorm.\" Ophthalmoscopy reveals dilated, non-reactive pupils, optic disc hyperemia, and retinal edema.\n5. **Severe Metabolic Acidosis:** High anion gap metabolic acidosis (HAGMA) presents with deep, rapid sighing respirations (Kussmaul breathing).\n\n---\n\n### Treatment Protocols\n1. **Airway & Resuscitation:** Secure the airway, administer oxygen, and support hemodynamics with IV fluids.\n2. **Correction of Acidosis:** Immediate administration of **Sodium Bicarbonate ($NaHCO_3$)** IV. This is vital to correct metabolic acidosis and promote the ionization of formic acid, preventing its entry into the brain and eyes and accelerating renal clearance.\n3. **Specific Antidotes:**\n   * **Fomepizole (4-methylpyrazole):** The preferred antidote. It is a potent inhibitor of alcohol dehydrogenase, preventing the formation of formic acid. Dose: 15 mg/kg IV loading dose.\n   * **Ethanol (Ethyl Alcohol):** Used if fomepizole is unavailable. Ethanol has a 10-20 times higher affinity for alcohol dehydrogenase than methanol, competitively inhibiting methanol metabolism. Maintain blood ethanol levels at 100-150 mg/dL.\n   * **Folinic Acid (Leucovorin) / Folic Acid:** Administered IV (50 mg every 4-6 hours) to enhance the breakdown of formic acid into carbon dioxide and water.\n4. **Hemodialysis:** Indicated if blood methanol levels are >50 mg/dL, in severe acidosis ($pH < 7.25$), or if visual symptoms are present.\n\n---\n\n### Postmortem Appearances\n#### External Findings\n* Deep cyanosis of the face, neck, and extremities.\n* Pinkish or cherry-red postmortem staining (due to elevated formic acid/undissociated oxyhemoglobin in some cases).\n* Froth at the mouth and nose; rigor mortis sets in early.\n\n#### Internal Findings\n* **Brain Necrosis:** The pathognomonic finding is **bilateral symmetric necrosis of the putamen / basal ganglia**. The brain is highly congested and edematous.\n* **Gastrointestinal:** Hemorrhagic gastritis, mucosal erosions of the stomach, and a highly congested, swollen pancreas.\n* **Ophthalmic:** Degeneration of the retinal ganglion cells and demyelination of the optic nerve.\n* **Viscera:** Severe congestion of the lungs, liver, and kidneys. Blood is dark, fluid, and may emit a faint, sweetish odor of methanol.",
