    "text": "What Is Widmark's Formula?",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "CNS Depressants",
    "answer": "### Definition\n**Widmark's formula** is a mathematical equation developed by Swedish chemist Erik M. P. Widmark. It is used in forensic toxicology to estimate the total quantity of absolute alcohol consumed by an individual or to estimate their Blood Alcohol Concentration (BAC) at a specific time.\n\n### The Equation\n$$A = c \\times p \\times r$$\n\nWhere:\n* **$A$:** Total weight of absolute alcohol absorbed/consumed (in grams).\n* **$c$:** Blood alcohol concentration (in g/kg or g/L).\n* **$p$:** Body weight of the individual (in kilograms).\n* **$r$:** Widmark's factor (the distribution ratio of alcohol between the tissues and blood). The average value is **0.68 for men** and **0.55 for women** (reflecting differences in body fat distribution).\n\n### Forensic Application\nIt is highly valuable in **retrograde extrapolation** to calculate the BAC of a driver at the time of a motor vehicle accident based on a blood sample taken hours later, by incorporating the average elimination rate of alcohol ($\\beta$-factor, typically **15 mg% per hour**).",
    "stars": 0
