    "id": 249,
    "text": "Wernickes Encephalopathy.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "CNS Depressants",
    "answer": "### Definition & Cause\n**Wernicke's encephalopathy** is an acute, life-threatening neuropsychiatric syndrome caused by a severe deficiency of **thiamine (Vitamin B1)**. It is most commonly observed in chronic alcoholics due to inadequate dietary intake, impaired intestinal absorption, and reduced hepatic storage of thiamine.\n\n### Pathophysiology\nThiamine is a coenzyme essential for glucose metabolism. A deficiency leads to cell death in vulnerable brain regions, particularly the mammillary bodies, thalamus, and periaqueductal grey matter.\n\n### Clinical Triad\n1. **Ocular Abnormalities:** Horizontal nystagmus, bilateral abducens nerve ($CN\\ VI$) palsy, and conjugate gaze palsy.\n2. **Ataxia:** A slow, unsteady, wide-based gait.\n3. **Global Confusion:** Disorientation, apathy, and memory impairment.\n\n### Treatment\nIt is a medical emergency requiring **immediate intravenous administration of thiamine** (usually 500 mg IV thrice daily). *Crucial Rule:* **Glucose must never be administered before thiamine**, as a glucose load can rapidly deplete the remaining thiamine reserves, precipitating irreversible brain damage or Korsakoff's psychosis.",
    "stars": 2
