    "id": 250,
    "text": "Rectified Spirit.",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "CNS Depressants",
    "answer": "### Definition & Composition\n**Rectified spirit** (also known as neutral spirit or rectified alcohol) is a highly concentrated form of ethanol that has been purified through repeated fractional distillation (rectification).\n\nIt typically contains **95% to 95.6% ethanol by volume** and approximately 4.4% to 5% water. It forms a constant boiling constant-composition azeotropic mixture, meaning it cannot be concentrated further by simple distillation.\n\n### Industrial and Medical Uses\nIt is widely used as an industrial solvent, in the manufacture of cosmetics, perfumes, and varnishes, in pharmaceutical tinctures, and as a raw material for producing diluted alcoholic beverages.\n\n### Forensic Significance\nIngestion of rectified spirit in its undiluted form is extremely hazardous. It acts as a local mucosal irritant, causing severe dehydration and congestion of the oral cavity and esophagus. It is rapidly absorbed, leading to acute, life-threatening alcohol poisoning characterized by rapid CNS depression, coma, and respiratory arrest.",
