    "id": 251,
    "text": "Saturday Night Palsy",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "CNS Depressants",
    "answer": "### Definition & Cause\n**Saturday Night Palsy** is a compressive neuropathy affecting the **radial nerve**, resulting in acute motor and sensory loss in the hand. It typically occurs in individuals who are heavily intoxicated with alcohol or sedatives.\n\n### Mechanism\nWhile in a deep, unresponsive sleep, the intoxicated person rests their upper arm awkwardly over a hard object, such as the backrest of a chair, the edge of a bed, or another hard surface. This causes prolonged, direct mechanical compression of the radial nerve against the humerus within the spiral groove, compounded by the alcohol-induced lack of normal protective postural changes.\n\n### Clinical Features\nThe classic presentation is **wrist drop** (inability to extend the wrist, thumb, and metacarpophalangeal joints) and sensory numbness or paresthesia over the dorsum of the hand, particularly the first web space.\n\n### Prognosis & Treatment\nThe condition is usually transient. Management involves physical therapy, wrist splinting, and avoidance of further compression, with full recovery typically occurring over weeks to months.",
