    "id": 252,
    "text": "Define Stupefying Agents. Describe The Signs, Symptoms, Treatment And MLI Of Datura Poisoning.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Deliriants",
    "answer": "### Definition of Stupefying Agents\n**Stupefying agents** are chemical substances or drugs that depress the central nervous system, causing drowsiness, confusion, loss of willpower, or complete unconsciousness. They are administered to victims, usually surreptitiously in food or drink, to facilitate crimes such as robbery, extortion, or sexual assault.\n\n---\n\n### Datura Poisoning\nDatura (*Datura stramonium* or *Datura fastuosa*) is a common wild plant in India. Its seeds and leaves contain anticholinergic alkaloids: **L-hyoscyamine, atropine, and scopolamine**.\n\n#### Signs & Symptoms\nThe clinical features of Datura poisoning are summarized by the classic anticholinergic mnemonic:\n* **Dry as a Bone:** Severe dryness of the mouth and throat, causing dysphagia (difficulty swallowing) and dysphonia (hoarse speech). Skin is hot and completely dry due to suppressed sweating.\n* **Red as a Beet:** Flushed face and neck due to cutaneous vasodilation.\n* **Blind as a Bat:** Widely dilated, fixed pupils (mydriasis) with photophobia and loss of accommodation.\n* **Hot as a Hare:** High fever (hyperthermia), which can reach 104-105°F.\n* **Mad as a Hatter:** Active delirium, visual and auditory hallucinations, muttering, and characteristic **carphologia** (picking at imaginary objects or threads on clothing).\n* **Full as a Flask:** Urinary retention due to detrusor muscle relaxation.\n\n#### Treatment\n* **Gastric Lavage:** Perform using dilute tannic acid or potassium permanganate. This is useful even hours after ingestion because atropine delays gastric emptying.\n* **Antidote:** **Physostigmine** (1-2 mg IV slowly). It is a tertiary acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that crosses the blood-brain barrier to reverse both central delirium and peripheral symptoms.\n* **Supportive:** Cold compresses for hyperthermia, urinary catheterization, and Diazepam to control severe delirium or convulsions.\n\n#### Medico-Legal Importance (MLI)\nDatura is the classic **stupefying agent** used by \"railway poisoners\" in India. The seeds are powdered and mixed with tea, food, or tobacco to rob unsuspecting travelers. Accidental poisoning is common in children who eat the fruit.",
