    "id": 255,
    "text": "Magnan's Symptoms/Cocaines Bugs..",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Deliriants",
    "answer": "### Definition & Cause\n**Magnan's symptom** (also known as **cocaine bugs** or **formication**) is a classic tactile hallucination experienced by chronic cocaine abusers (cocainism).\n\n### Pathophysiology\nIt arises due to the hyper-stimulation of peripheral sensory nerves and altered central dopaminergic pathways caused by chronic, high-dose cocaine abuse.\n\n### Clinical Features\nThe patient experiences a highly distressing, false sensation of tiny insects, bugs, lice, sand grains, or crystals crawling on, just beneath, or through their skin.\n\n### Patient Behavior\nTo rid themselves of these imaginary bugs, patients frantically scratch, pick at, or cut their skin with needles, fingernails, or knives. This results in characteristic skin excoriations, chronic ulcerations, and self-inflicted wounds, often referred to as **\"cocaine sores.\"** It is a pathognomonic sign of chronic cocaine toxicity and is frequently accompanied by paranoid delusions and microptic visual hallucinations (seeing real objects as extremely small).",
