    "id": 257,
    "text": "LSD",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Deliriants",
    "answer": "### Identity & Source\n**Lysergic Acid Diethylamide (LSD)**, commonly known as \"Acid,\" is a highly potent, semi-synthetic hallucinogen derived from lysergic acid, an alkaloid found in **ergot** (the fungus *Claviceps purpurea* which infects rye grains).\n\n### Mechanism of Action\nLSD acts as a partial agonist at central **serotonin $5-HT_{2A}$ receptors**, profoundly disrupting normal sensory processing and cortical function.\n\n### Clinical Features\n* **Hallucinations:** Vivid, colorful, geometric visual hallucinations and illusions.\n* **Synesthesia:** A classic phenomenon where sensory modalities merge (e.g., \"hearing colors\" or \"seeing sounds\").\n* **Psychological Effects:** Depersonalization, a distorted sense of time and space, panic attacks (\"bad trips\"), and suicidal tendencies.\n* **Flashbacks:** Spontaneous, transient recurrences of the LSD-induced hallucinatory experience months or years after the last dose, occurring without drug ingestion.\n\n### Forensic Significance\nLSD has a very high safety margin; direct fatal poisoning is extremely rare. Deaths are almost exclusively accidental or suicidal, resulting from dangerous, uninhibited behavior during hallucinatory \"bad trips.\"",
