    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Asphyxiants",
    "answer": "### Mechanism of Action (MOA)\nCarbon monoxide (CO) binds to the iron atom of hemoglobin with an affinity **200 to 250 times greater than oxygen**, forming **carboxyhemoglobin (COHb)**. This severely reduces the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood and shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the left, preventing oxygen release to tissues and causing profound cellular hypoxia.\n\n### Signs & Symptoms\nManifests as a throbbing headache (the earliest and most classic symptom), dizziness, nausea, confusion, cherry-red skin and mucous membranes, dyspnea, seizures, coma, and death.\n\n### Treatment\nRemove the patient from the source. Administer **100% high-flow normobaric oxygen** via a non-rebreather mask (which reduces the half-life of COHb from 5 hours to 80 minutes). **Hyperbaric oxygen (HBO) therapy** is indicated in severe cases (e.g., pregnancy, coma, $COHb > 25\\%$).\n\n### Postmortem Findings\n1. **Cherry-Red Coloration:** Distinctive cherry-red postmortem staining, muscles, blood, and internal organs.\n2. **Brain Lesions:** Bilateral necrotic patches in the **globus pallidus** of the basal ganglia.\n3. **Lungs:** Heavily congested, edematous, and bright red.",
