    "id": 259,
    "text": "Describe The MOA, Signs, Symptoms , Treatment& Postmortem Findings In A Case Of Cyanide Poisoning(CN).",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Asphyxiants",
    "answer": "### Mechanism of Action (MOA)\nCyanide ($CN^-$) binds to the ferric ($Fe^{3+}$) ion of **cytochrome oxidase a3** within the mitochondrial electron transport chain. This halts cellular respiration, preventing the utilization of oxygen and stopping ATP production. Cells shift to anaerobic metabolism, resulting in acute lactic acidosis and death from **histotoxic hypoxia**.\n\n### Signs & Symptoms\nSymptoms occur within seconds to minutes: headache, hyperpnea, a characteristic **bitter almond odor** on the breath, convulsions, coma, and rapid cardiorespiratory arrest.\n\n### Treatment\n1. **Hydroxocobalamin (Cyanokit):** The preferred antidote; binds cyanide to form non-toxic cyanocobalamin ($Vit\\ B_{12}$), excreted in urine.\n2. **Cyanide Antidote Kit:** Inhaled *Amyl Nitrite* and IV *Sodium Nitrite* to induce methemoglobinemia (which binds cyanide, pulling it away from cytochrome oxidase), followed by IV *Sodium Thiosulfate* to convert cyanide into non-toxic thiocyanate via the rhodanese enzyme.\n\n### Postmortem Findings\n1. **Postmortem Staining:** Bright brick-red hypostasis due to high oxygen saturation in venous blood.\n2. **Odor:** Pronounced bitter almond odor from the skull cavity and stomach contents.\n3. **Stomach:** Highly congested and eroded gastric mucosa with altered red blood.",
