    "text": "STRYCHNINE POISONING -MOA,Signs & Symptoms & Treatment (5 M)",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Cardiac & Spinal Poisons",
    "answer": "### Mechanism of Action (MOA)\nStrychnine (the toxic alkaloid from the seeds of ***Strychnos nux-vomica***) acts as a selective, competitive **antagonist of glycine**, which is a major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the spinal cord and brainstem. By blocking glycine receptors on post-synaptic membranes, strychnine removes normal reflex inhibition, leading to unchecked sensory-induced hyperexcitation of motor neurons.\n\n### Signs & Symptoms\nMinimal sensory stimuli (light, sound, or touch) trigger sudden, violent, painful tetanic convulsions:\n* **Opisthotonus:** Severe spasms of back muscles, arching the body like a bow so the patient rests only on their head and heels.\n* **Risus Sardonicus:** Spasm of facial muscles producing a characteristic grimacing grin.\n* **Consciousness:** **Fully intact** throughout the convulsions, making the experience extremely terrifying. Death occurs due to asphyxia from spasm of the respiratory muscles.\n\n### Treatment\n1. **Sensory Isolation:** Place the patient in a dark, quiet, soundproof room to prevent triggering convulsions.\n2. **Anticonvulsants:** **Diazepam** IV is the first-line therapy to control spasms.\n3. **Airway Management:** Intubate and administer neuromuscular blockers (e.g., vecuronium) to paralyze muscles and protect ventilation. Gastric lavage is contraindicated during active spasms.",
