    "id": 269,
    "text": "Lathyrism",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Pharmacological Toxicology & Food Poisoning",
    "answer": "### Definition & Cause\n**Lathyrism** is a neurological disease (neurolathyrism) characterized by spastic paraplegia. It is caused by the excessive and prolonged consumption of **Khesari dhal (*Lathyrus sativus*)** as a staple dietary source, a situation commonly observed in rural populations during droughts or famines.\n\n### Active Principle & Pathophysiology\nThe toxic principle in Khesari dhal is an amino acid analogue known as **BOAA (Beta-N-oxalyl-amino-L-alanine)** or **ODAP**. ODAP acts as a potent agonist at AMPA glutamate receptors, inducing excitotoxic damage and selective degeneration of upper motor neurons in the pyramidal tracts of the spinal cord.\n\n### Clinical Features\nIt presents as a slowly progressive, irreversible **spastic paraparesis of the lower limbs**. Early signs include stiffness and a heavy feeling in the legs, progressing to a characteristic **\"scissor gait\"** or \"crossed-leg gait.\" In advanced cases, the patient requires walking sticks or is completely crippled.\n\n### Preventive Measures\nPreventive strategies include parboiling the pulses in water before cooking (which leaches out the water-soluble BOAA toxin) and cultivating genetically modified low-ODAP strains of *Lathyrus sativus*.",
