    "text": "Aphrodisiacs/Love Philter With Examples",
    "type": "VSAQ",
    "chapter": "Pharmacological Toxicology & Food Poisoning",
    "answer": "### Definitions\n* **Aphrodisiacs:** Chemical substances or preparations claimed to stimulate, enhance, or restore sexual desire, arousal, or performance.\n* **Love Philters (Love Potions):** Magical or chemical mixtures traditionally prepared to be administered surreptitiously to a person to make them fall in love with or become sexually attracted to another.\n\n### Mechanism & Toxicity\nNo true medical aphrodisiacs exist in the form of potions. Most historical agents act either by causing local urogenital tract irritation, pelvic vascular congestion, or central nervous system disinhibition. These substances possess a very narrow therapeutic index and frequently cause severe systemic toxicity.\n\n### Examples\n1. **Cantharidin (Spanish Fly):** Blister beetle extract. It causes intense pelvic congestion and painful, persistent erection (priapism), but also severe hemorrhagic gastroenteritis and acute renal failure.\n2. **Yohimbine:** An alpha-2 adrenergic blocker. It increases genital blood flow but can cause dangerous hypertension, anxiety, and tachycardia.\n3. **Alcohol & Cannabis:** Act by lowering psychological inhibitions and disinhibiting behavior, though they ultimately impair physical sexual performance.",
